/* GENERATED by tools/import_book.js from the finished manuscript of Book 5. Story text is the author's, verbatim; do not edit here. */
(function(){ G.HFQ.addSeriesText("htfa-5",{"chapters":[{"t":"Prologue — The Line That Wouldn’t Turn Green","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing Captain Rowan Pike said to me that Monday was, “You’re early.”"},{"p":"I looked at the clock over the reception desk. “Seven minutes.”"},{"p":"“That’s a measurement, not an explanation.”"},{"p":"I had hoped for *Good morning, Lincoln*. Maybe even *Nice to see you again*. I would have settled for a nod that didn’t seem to require supporting documents."},{"p":"“I wanted time to get organized,” I said."},{"p":"Pike’s gaze dropped to the folder under my arm. Its corners were already soft from being opened, closed, and reopened at our kitchen table. Inside were the route-verification worksheets I’d spent three evenings revising, a list of questions for Mara, and a page titled THINGS I WILL NOT ASSUME, which Dad had called my autobiography."},{"p":"“Organized,” Pike repeated. “Or reassured?”"},{"p":"“Is both an option?”"},{"p":"“Sometimes.” He held open the door to the briefing room. “Not always.”"},{"p":"The room smelled faintly of dry-erase markers and the burnt coffee somebody had abandoned near the printer. On the wall was a clock showing Zulu time. On the table lay a sectional chart, a printed weather briefing, and a sheet of paper turned face down."},{"p":"My eyes went straight to the paper."},{"p":"Pike noticed. He had the attention span of an airport security camera, except I suspected the cameras blinked more often."},{"p":"“That’s not your test,” he said."},{"p":"“Wasn’t asking.”"},{"p":"“Your face was.”"},{"p":"I sat down. The chair squeaked. For a second, I wondered whether that counted against me."},{"p":"My last evaluation had ended with the word *conditional* in red pencil. I had spotted a bad alternate-airport choice and questioned missing weather information, but I’d missed a route restriction tucked into the scenario. Pike hadn’t shouted. Somehow that had made it worse. He’d asked me to explain my verification process, and I had discovered I didn’t have one. I had habits that worked until they didn’t."},{"p":"I’d spent most of the weeks since then trying to build something better."},{"p":"Pike sat opposite me and opened his notebook. “Tell me what changed.”"},{"p":"I could have given him the entire folder. It would have been easier than answering."},{"p":"“I stopped checking only the things I expected to find,” I said. “Now I start with the proposed route, then I check the current chart, relevant airspace and restrictions, weather, airports, and notices that could affect the flight. I write down what I verified, what source I used, and when I checked it.”"},{"p":"“And when you find a problem?”"},{"p":"“I don’t fix the paperwork so it looks clean. I stop and reconsider the route.”"},{"p":"“And if you can’t resolve it?”"},{"p":"“I ask for help. Or I don’t recommend the flight.”"},{"p":"Pike tapped his pencil once. “That is a better answer than last time.”"},{"p":"Relief warmed my face."},{"p":"“It’s also an answer,” he continued, “not a demonstration.”"},{"p":"There went the relief."},{"p":"He slid the face-down page across the table. It contained a fictional route, two possible destinations, and a handful of details that looked harmless until you read the fine print. At the bottom, in a square outlined in red, was a single sentence:"},{"p":"*Do not begin until you know what information you still need.*"},{"p":"I reached for my pencil, then stopped."},{"p":"For once, stopping felt like the beginning of the work."}]},{"t":"Chapter One — The Folder with the Bent Corner","blocks":[{"p":"I used to think there were two kinds of people at an airport: the ones who knew what they were doing and the ones who stared at signs until somebody rescued them."},{"p":"By the time I was fifteen, I had discovered a third kind. People who knew enough to realize how much they could still get wrong."},{"p":"Unfortunately, I had become one of them."},{"p":"Pike’s worksheet stayed in front of me for nearly twenty minutes. It described a made-up training route between two fictional airports, with an alternate that appeared convenient on the map. The weather met the exercise’s stated minimums. The fuel numbers seemed generous. A note at the top said the route had been planned the previous evening."},{"p":"That last part bothered me."},{"p":"I wrote CURRENT? beside the chart reference and circled the weather briefing’s issue time. Then I made a list of information the exercise didn’t provide: whether the chart and navigation data were current, what notices applied, whether any restrictions affected the proposed route, and whether the alternate’s facilities and conditions were suitable for the aircraft and the training objective."},{"p":"Pike watched without helping. He could make silence feel like an extra question."},{"p":"“Finished?” he asked when I put my pencil down."},{"p":"“Not with a flight recommendation.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because I haven’t verified enough to make one.”"},{"p":"He glanced at the sheet. “The route looks reasonable.”"},{"p":"“That’s what got me last time.”"},{"p":"He turned the page and revealed a second sheet containing fictional updates. One notice affected an area near the original route, but only during certain hours. Another concerned equipment at an airport the route didn’t use. A weather observation was newer than the forecast summary in the packet."},{"p":"My first instinct was to grab the notice about the route and declare the problem solved. Instead, I checked its effective time against the proposed departure. Then I checked the location. The restriction was relevant to our imaginary flight, but not in the way I’d assumed from the heading."},{"p":"I crossed out my first note and wrote a more careful one."},{"p":"Pike leaned back. “What did you just do?”"},{"p":"“Changed my mind.”"},{"p":"“About?”"},{"p":"“Which notice mattered. I saw the heading and jumped.”"},{"p":"“Did the heading make you wrong?”"},{"p":"“No. Not reading the rest of it did.”"},{"p":"His pencil hovered over the notebook. “Continue.”"},{"p":"By the end of the exercise, I had a list of conditions that would need confirmation before any real flight could be planned. I also had three eraser crumbs stuck to my sleeve and a sore spot on my thumb from pressing too hard."},{"p":"Pike collected the worksheet without showing me his notes."},{"p":"“Is that it?” I asked."},{"p":"“For today.”"},{"p":"“Did I pass?”"},{"p":"“Did I tell you this was the reassessment?”"},{"p":"I stared at him."},{"p":"“No,” I admitted."},{"p":"“Then why would I grade it as one?”"},{"p":"It was a fair question. I disliked it intensely."},{"p":"Outside the briefing room, Mara Ellery stood beside the vending machine holding a paper cup of tea. She took one look at me and raised an eyebrow."},{"p":"“He gave me a worksheet that wasn’t a test,” I said."},{"p":"“Terrible. A teacher teaching.”"},{"p":"“He could have warned me.”"},{"p":"“He did. You just wanted a different warning.”"},{"p":"I walked with her toward the hangars. The morning air smelled of wet grass and warm pavement, and somewhere a piston engine coughed itself awake. A Cessna taxied past the far side of the ramp, its propeller making the background shimmer."},{"p":"“Pike said my process is better,” I told her."},{"p":"“That sounds promising.”"},{"p":"“But he didn’t clear the condition.”"},{"p":"“Was he supposed to today?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"Mara sipped her tea. She didn’t smile, which was considerate of her."},{"p":"“He’s involved in the navigation fellowship,” she said, “and his standards matter there. But your flight training with me has its own requirements. One evaluation doesn’t turn into every evaluation.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Do you?”"},{"p":"I kicked a pebble away from the walkway. “I’m trying to figure out whether I’m getting better or just getting better at explaining mistakes.”"},{"p":"Mara stopped by the hangar door. “That’s worth thinking about. But don’t confuse being able to name a mistake with making the same one forever.”"},{"p":"Inside, the Cessna 172S waited with its wings casting long shadows on the floor. I had flown it with Mara enough times that its shape felt familiar. Not simple. Not mine. Familiar."},{"p":"On the wall behind it hung a whiteboard listing the week’s lessons. My name appeared beside *dual instruction— airwork and traffic pattern review*. Underneath, in Mara’s tidy handwriting, she’d added *schedule subject to weather, aircraft availability, and readiness*."},{"p":"I pointed. “You write that every week.”"},{"p":"“Because it’s true every week.”"},{"p":"“Very inspirational.”"},{"p":"“I have a poster somewhere with a sunset on it.”"},{"p":"Before I could ask whether the sunset was also subject to aircraft availability, my phone buzzed."},{"p":"Rafe had sent a photograph of himself wearing a headset that looked enormous on his head. The message read: *COMMUNICATIONS CLUB ORIENTATION. I have achieved professional ear coverage.*"},{"p":"A second message arrived: *They let me listen to a practice radio exercise. I understood at least sixty percent. Possibly forty. Numbers are complicated.*"},{"p":"I grinned and typed: *Congratulations. Please don’t accidentally clear me to land.*"},{"p":"His reply came immediately: *I only issue snack clearances.*"},{"p":"For a while, Rafe’s disappointment about my fellowship nomination had made every conversation feel like walking through a room full of glass cups. We’d apologized, though not in the same way or on the same day. Now he had something that belonged to him, and I was glad. Also a little jealous that he got to wear a headset the size of a satellite dish."},{"p":"I found Imani and Beckett at a folding table near the classroom entrance. Imani had three color-coded tabs sticking out of a weather notebook. Beckett was holding a small plastic gear between his fingertips, looking offended by it."},{"p":"“This gear,” he announced, “has betrayed engineering.”"},{"p":"“What did it do?” I asked."},{"p":"“It fits perfectly until I attach the thing it’s supposed to turn.”"},{"p":"“So it works as long as you don’t use it?”"},{"p":"“Exactly. A very successful design for a museum.”"},{"p":"Imani looked up. “How’d Pike go?”"},{"p":"I pulled out the bent-corner folder. “He gave me an exercise and refused to call it an exercise that counted.”"},{"p":"“Did you learn something?” she asked."},{"p":"“Several irritating things.”"},{"p":"“Then it counted for something.”"},{"p":"She returned to her notebook. She didn’t ask about my score or try to make the morning sound better than it was. Since the trouble between us over her grandmother’s privacy, I’d learned to appreciate the questions Imani chose not to ask."},{"p":"“How’s Nana June’s tomato empire?” I asked."},{"p":"“Expanding. She says if you come over, you’re assigned to carry dirt.”"},{"p":"“Tell her I have aviation responsibilities.”"},{"p":"“She says dirt has a better attendance record.”"},{"p":"Beckett held up his gear. “Does she need a mechanical tomato harvester?”"},{"p":"“Not one you built,” Imani said."},{"p":"He pressed a hand to his chest. “This group has no faith.”"},{"p":"I sat down with them. For the first time all morning, the folder stayed closed."},{"p":"A few minutes later, Mara came over with a sheet of paper divided into four columns. At the top was a title: *Your Own Next Step*."},{"p":"“Rafe’s exploring communications,” she said. “Beckett has been asking about maintenance and mechanical systems. Imani’s building a weather-observation project. Lincoln has the fellowship. None of those paths needs to look exactly like the others.”"},{"p":"Beckett inspected the sheet. “Can my next step be fixing a gear that hates me?”"},{"p":"“An excellent first step,” Mara said. “Write down what you need to learn before you change anything.”"},{"p":"I looked at my empty column."},{"p":"For weeks I’d been measuring my progress by a single red mark on Pike’s evaluation. It hadn’t occurred to me that the others were measuring theirs with different tools."},{"p":"At the bottom of my page, I wrote: *Get better at checking before deciding.*"},{"p":"Then, after a moment, I added: *Ask Mara what comes next for flight training.*"},{"p":"It was the second line that made my stomach do something strange."},{"p":"My sixteenth birthday wasn’t far away. I’d known the number for years: sixteen was the minimum age to solo a powered airplane in the United States. But a birthday wasn’t a clearance. It wasn’t a logbook endorsement, a medical qualification, or proof I could handle an airplane without Mara beside me."},{"p":"For the first time, the difference between being old enough and being ready felt less like a fact from a textbook and more like a door I might someday stand in front of."}]},{"t":"Chapter Two — The Seat Beside Me","blocks":[{"p":"The following Thursday, I arrived at the airport with two things in my backpack: a notebook full of questions and a peanut-butter sandwich Dad had wrapped in foil so thoroughly that I could have used it to reflect sunlight into space."},{"p":"Mara was checking the training schedule when I found her."},{"p":"“Question,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s usually how these begin.”"},{"p":"“When do we start working toward solo?”"},{"p":"She set down her pen. Not dramatically. Just enough to give me her full attention."},{"p":"“We’ve been working toward independence since your first lesson,” she said. “But specific solo preparation involves a lot more than a date on a calendar. We build proficiency, judgment, consistency, and the required knowledge and endorsements. You’ll need to meet the applicable student-pilot and medical requirements, too.”"},{"p":"“I know sixteen isn’t automatic.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"“I just want to know what the next steps look like.”"},{"p":"“That’s a better question.”"},{"p":"She pulled a blank sheet from the printer and drew three boxes. In the first she wrote *skills*. In the second, *judgment*. In the third, *requirements*."},{"p":"“Today we can work on the first two,” she said. “The third includes things we will verify carefully as the time approaches. And there is no promise of a particular solo date.”"},{"p":"I nodded, trying not to look disappointed by a promise she hadn’t made."},{"p":"We reviewed the weather, the airplane’s status, and the planned lesson. Mara judged the conditions suitable for our lesson, then walked me through the go/no-go decision instead of handing me the answer. I completed the preflight inspection under her supervision, following the aircraft’s checklist and bringing a question about a tire to her attention instead of deciding it looked fine because I wanted to leave."},{"p":"“Good catch,” she said after examining it."},{"p":"“Is it a problem?”"},{"p":"“It’s a question worth checking. Those are different things.”"},{"p":"She consulted the appropriate aircraft information and confirmed the condition was acceptable before we continued. I made a note in my book. Not because I thought I could memorize my way around every possible tire question, but because I wanted to remember the pause."},{"p":"We climbed aboard. Mara settled into the right seat, fastened her harness, and looked over at me."},{"p":"“Your airplane for the portions I assign,” she said. “My responsibility throughout. If I need control, I’ll say so.”"},{"p":"I repeated the transfer-of-controls expectations back to her. The words were familiar. That didn’t make them less important."},{"p":"The engine started, the instruments came alive, and the airplane became a place with work to do rather than a shape on a hangar floor."},{"p":"During the flight, Mara had me practice the fundamentals we’d been building for years: coordinated control, maintaining assigned headings and altitudes, scanning outside and inside, and keeping track of what was happening around us. She managed the lesson, selected the maneuvers, and intervened when necessary. There was no dramatic emergency and no sudden declaration that I was ready to fly alone."},{"p":"The hardest part was ordinary."},{"p":"On one heading, I let my altitude wander while concentrating on a distant landmark. On another, I corrected a little too eagerly and spent several seconds undoing my own correction."},{"p":"“Look farther ahead,” Mara said. “Give yourself time to notice the change before it becomes a big one.”"},{"p":"I tried again. This time I saw the trend sooner."},{"p":"“Better,” she said."},{"p":"I liked that word. Better didn’t mean perfect. It also didn’t mean imaginary."},{"p":"After we landed and taxied back, Mara asked me to name two things I’d done well and two things I’d work on."},{"p":"“Stayed aware of the assigned area,” I said. “And I caught my heading drift sooner near the end.”"},{"p":"“Agreed.”"},{"p":"“I chased altitude when I got distracted. And I need to make smaller, more patient corrections.”"},{"p":"“Also agreed.”"},{"p":"I waited for her to add something about solo."},{"p":"She didn’t."},{"p":"Finally I asked, “So where does that put me?”"},{"p":"“Here,” she said, tapping the day’s lesson record. “One lesson farther along, with specific things to practice next.”"},{"p":"I groaned. “You and Pike have a secret competition to see who can give the least satisfying answer.”"},{"p":"“I’m winning,” she said."},{"p":"In the classroom afterward, I found a message from Imani. It was a photo of a hand-drawn chart with temperatures and wind observations from Nana June’s backyard. The lines were neat, the labels careful. In the corner, Nana June had written: *THE TOMATOES DISAGREE WITH YOUR FORECAST.*"},{"p":"I replied: *Are the tomatoes certified observers?*"},{"p":"*They have more field experience than you,* Imani wrote."},{"p":"I laughed, then looked at the paper Mara had given me. Skills. Judgment. Requirements. I hadn’t filled the boxes with answers yet. But at least I understood why there were three."},{"p":"Rafe called as I was packing up. He talked for six straight minutes about a communications-club mentor who had shown him how a misunderstood radio call could turn an easy exercise into a confusing one. He was planning to help organize a practice session for new members."},{"p":"“Are you going to let anyone else talk?” I asked."},{"p":"“Eventually. The training schedule is flexible.”"},{"p":"“Very generous.”"},{"p":"“Hey, Lincoln?” His voice changed, just a little. “I’m glad you got the fellowship. I don’t think I said that right before.”"},{"p":"I sat down again. Through the window, I could see the Cessna parked where we’d left it, still and ordinary in the afternoon sun."},{"p":"“Thanks,” I said. “I’m glad you found the radio group.”"},{"p":"“Me too. Apparently I can be interested in airplanes without wanting to do exactly what you do.”"},{"p":"“Disturbing discovery.”"},{"p":"“Tell me about it.”"},{"p":"When the call ended, I opened my notebook to the page with my solo questions. I added one more at the bottom:"},{"p":"*What would I do differently if nobody were sitting beside me to catch the mistake?*"},{"p":"I stared at it for a while."},{"p":"Then I drew a box around it and left the answer blank."},{"p":"That evening, a new message appeared in my fellowship account. It came from Captain Pike, with Mara copied. The subject line read: *Navigation Lab Orientation — Additional Evaluation Sequence*."},{"p":"I opened it at the kitchen table while Dad unloaded groceries and Mom sorted a stack of mail."},{"p":"The message confirmed my place at the advanced navigation lab. It also said that my conditional route-verification result remained in effect. Before participating in certain advanced scenarios, I’d complete a separate reassessment with Pike. My regular flight training would continue with Mara under its own standards and requirements."},{"p":"At the bottom was a schedule for a team exercise with students I hadn’t met."},{"p":"I read the names twice. None was Imani, Beckett, or Rafe."},{"p":"“Everything okay?” Mom asked."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said, then considered the question. “Mostly. There’s a new group. And the reassessment still isn’t scheduled.”"},{"p":"Mom set the mail down. “Are you excited?”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“Nervous?”"},{"p":"“Also yes.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “Sounds like something worth doing, then. As long as we work out the practical pieces.”"},{"p":"Dad held up my foil-wrapped sandwich, which I’d forgotten in the backpack. “Is this a science experiment?”"},{"p":"“Lunch.”"},{"p":"“From what century?”"},{"p":"“Please don’t open it.”"},{"p":"He placed it on the counter with exaggerated care. “Aviation has enough mysteries.”"},{"p":"I looked back at Pike’s message. A year ago, I would have seen the unfamiliar names and immediately wondered how to prove I belonged. Now I wondered what they might notice that I wouldn’t."},{"p":"It wasn’t a complete transformation. I still wanted to do well. I still wanted Pike to erase that red condition. And when I pictured an airplane taking off without an instructor in the other seat, my heart jumped ahead of every sensible thought in my head."},{"p":"But I closed the message without pretending the next step was already mine."},{"p":"Tomorrow, I would start preparing for it."}]},{"t":"Chapter Three — The Airport That Looked Familiar","blocks":[{"p":"On the morning of the navigation lab, Dad put a banana in my backpack and told me not to mistake it for an emergency locator transmitter."},{"p":"“Different shapes,” I said."},{"p":"“Both yellow. Both important. One works better with peanut butter.”"},{"p":"Mom stood by the door holding the permission packet. “You have your notebook?”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“Pencils?”"},{"p":"“Three.”"},{"p":"“Lunch?”"},{"p":"“Assuming Dad hasn’t installed it in the airplane.”"},{"p":"She glanced at the clock. “And you understand that the lab is not your reassessment?”"},{"p":"I stopped adjusting my backpack strap. “I understand.”"},{"p":"Mostly. Captain Pike had written it plainly in the orientation message: participation in introductory group exercises did not clear my conditional route-verification result. My reassessment would happen later, when he was satisfied I’d practiced enough to demonstrate a dependable process. I could explain that to anyone who asked. It didn’t stop a small part of me from hoping he’d see me do something brilliant and decide the paperwork was unnecessary."},{"p":"Mom seemed to read the thought anyway. “Have a good day,” she said. “Not a perfect one.”"},{"p":"“That sounds suspiciously like advice.”"},{"p":"“Then consider it a warning.”"},{"p":"The fellowship lab occupied two classrooms beside a maintenance hangar at Hampton Roads Executive. A folding sign by the door said REGIONAL YOUTH NAVIGATION FELLOWSHIP, with an arrow pointing in a direction that seemed unnecessarily confident for a navigation program."},{"p":"Inside, four students were already gathered around a table. One wore a green sweatshirt with a model airplane pinned to the collar. Another had spread her pencils in a perfectly straight row. A third was attempting to get a paper cup out of a dispenser without taking the entire stack."},{"p":"“Lincoln Burgess?” asked the girl with the pencils."},{"p":"“Unless something went terribly wrong with the name tags.”"},{"p":"“I’m Celia Ward.” She slid a blank worksheet toward me. “We’re supposed to introduce ourselves and list one strength.”"},{"p":"“Do you get extra points for being modest?”"},{"p":"“No,” she said, without smiling. “But you might lose points for taking too long.”"},{"p":"The student in the green sweatshirt laughed. “I’m Marcus. I draw maps badly and fix bicycles pretty well.”"},{"p":"The paper-cup student finally freed one. “Nico. My strength is defeating the cup dispenser.”"},{"p":"The fourth student, a quiet boy named Ellis, said he liked weather patterns. I told them I had experience with charts and supervised flight lessons, and that I was trying to become less talented at jumping to conclusions."},{"p":"“That’s an unusual strength,” Celia said."},{"p":"“It’s still under construction.”"},{"p":"Captain Pike entered with a woman I hadn’t met. She had close-cropped silver hair, a navy jacket, and a voice that carried without getting loud."},{"p":"“This is Ms. Alvarez,” Pike said. “She coordinates the fellowship’s ground activities. Today you are planners, not pilots. Your task is to prepare a recommendation for a fictional daylight VFR training flight. No one will fly the route you produce.”"},{"p":"Ms. Alvarez distributed scenario packets. The proposed trip used invented airport names and deliberately simplified distances. Every packet included a sectional-style training chart, airport details, weather reports with observation times, and a page of operational notes."},{"p":"“Your recommendation must be conditional,” she said. “Identify what you know, what needs confirmation, and what would change your decision. You have forty-five minutes.”"},{"p":"Celia took charge of the chart. “Let’s start with the direct route.”"},{"p":"Marcus pulled the airport sheet toward him. “I can check the destination.”"},{"p":"“I’ll look at the weather,” Ellis said."},{"p":"Nico raised his cup. “I can supervise hydration.”"},{"p":"“You can check the operational notes,” Celia replied."},{"p":"I studied the chart. The destination, fictional Westbridge Field, sat just beyond a reservoir. I’d seen a similar airport symbol in one of Pike’s practice packets. The runway lengths looked comfortable for the exercise. The weather was good at the departure field. My brain began arranging a tidy recommendation before the others had finished opening their pages."},{"p":"“Westbridge should work,” I said. “If the destination weather holds, we could—”"},{"p":"“Hold on,” Ellis interrupted. “Which report are you using?”"},{"p":"“The one on the front page.”"},{"p":"“That’s the departure report.”"},{"p":"I looked again. He was right. The destination observation was on the next sheet, and it was older."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “I got ahead of myself.”"},{"p":"Celia pointed to the reservoir. “I thought you said you’d flown around here.”"},{"p":"“Not here. These are invented places.”"},{"p":"“I meant the region.”"},{"p":"“Some of it. Not this airport.”"},{"p":"She held my gaze for a moment, then returned to the chart. “Let’s stick with what’s in the packet.”"},{"p":"Her tone wasn’t rude. It still stung. I had wanted my experience to help. Instead, I’d made myself sound like the person who knew the answer before reading the question."},{"p":"We worked in relative quiet. Marcus found that Westbridge’s runway information was incomplete for the aircraft in the scenario. Ellis marked the weather report’s age in the margin. Nico found a note about a temporary restriction affecting part of the proposed route during a specified time window. He circled it twice, then slid it toward me."},{"p":"“Is this the kind of thing you were talking about in introductions?” he asked."},{"p":"My stomach tightened. It was almost exactly the kind of detail I’d missed in my earlier evaluation."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “And I’m glad you found it.”"},{"p":"Celia looked over. “Would the restriction affect us?”"},{"p":"“We need to compare its location, altitude limits, effective time, and our proposed route,” I said. “We shouldn’t guess from the headline.”"},{"p":"For the next ten minutes we compared the scenario notes with the chart and sketched two possible routes. One avoided the restricted area on paper, but passed closer to a patch of weather the forecast suggested could deteriorate. The other took longer and raised questions about fuel margins. We didn’t have all the information needed to recommend either without conditions."},{"p":"“We’re supposed to pick one,” Celia said, tapping the assignment heading."},{"p":"“It says prepare a recommendation,” Ellis replied."},{"p":"“A recommendation that says we don’t know?”"},{"p":"“Maybe a recommendation that says what we’d need to know,” I said."},{"p":"Celia leaned back. “That sounds like a way to avoid being wrong.”"},{"p":"I felt the old urge to argue. I’d spent enough time with Pike to assemble an impressive speech about uncertainty. The speech was already halfway to my mouth when I noticed Celia looking at the clock."},{"p":"She wasn’t trying to win an argument. She was worried we’d run out of time with nothing to present."},{"p":"“What if we make a decision tree?” I suggested. “If the updated destination weather meets the instructor’s criteria, and the route and airport information check out, here’s the route we’d propose. If not, here’s our alternate recommendation. And if the missing information stays missing, no departure recommendation.”"},{"p":"Marcus nodded. “I can put the distance and fuel comparisons next to each branch.”"},{"p":"“Ellis can list the weather questions,” Nico said. “I’ll list the restrictions.”"},{"p":"Celia looked at the worksheet, then at us. “I can make it readable.”"},{"p":"“You can make anything readable,” Marcus said. “Your handwriting has its own zip code.”"},{"p":"For the first time, Celia smiled."},{"p":"We finished with four minutes left. When Ms. Alvarez came around, Celia presented our recommendation. Marcus explained the estimates. Ellis described the missing weather update. Nico pointed out the route note. I explained why we had left the final go/no-go decision conditional on verified information and the instructor’s judgment."},{"p":"Pike listened without interrupting. Then he asked, “Who found the route restriction?”"},{"p":"“Nico,” I said."},{"p":"“Who identified the weather-report problem?”"},{"p":"“Ellis.”"},{"p":"“Who decided how to present the options?”"},{"p":"“Celia,” I said. “We worked it out together.”"},{"p":"Pike studied the page. “And what did you contribute?”"},{"p":"The question shouldn’t have been hard. A year earlier I would have listed everything I’d touched, including the paper clips."},{"p":"“I suggested the decision tree,” I said. “And I stopped us from treating a familiar-looking airport as a verified choice. After Ellis corrected me.”"},{"p":"Pike made a note. I tried not to read it upside down."},{"p":"After the session, Celia caught up with me by the vending machines."},{"p":"“I was short with you earlier,” she said."},{"p":"“A little.”"},{"p":"“You sounded like you’d already decided before the rest of us started.”"},{"p":"“I had,” I admitted. “That was the problem.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “You were better once you listened.”"},{"p":"“I’m going to have that printed on a shirt.”"},{"p":"“Get one for Nico that says CUP SUPERVISOR.”"},{"p":"We walked back toward the classroom, where Nico was trying to persuade Marcus that the dispenser had deliberately challenged him."},{"p":"I still wanted Pike to notice my work. But when I thought about what had gone well, the best part wasn’t the decision tree. It was that we’d noticed things I would have missed alone."},{"p":"On my phone, a message from Beckett waited beneath a photograph of a crooked metal bracket he’d made in the workshop."},{"p":"*Prototype survived. My thumb did not enjoy the process. How was your fancy map club?*"},{"p":"I typed back: *We didn’t get lost on paper.*"},{"p":"Then I added: *Mostly because other people were paying attention.*"},{"p":"It was a better answer than I’d expected to have that morning."}]},{"t":"Chapter Four — A Good Landing Isn’t a Promise","blocks":[{"p":"The next Tuesday, the airport smelled like wet pavement and somebody’s breakfast sandwich. Low clouds had cleared by midmorning, leaving a bright strip of sky over the hangars. I was halfway through telling Mara about the navigation lab when she pointed at my shoes."},{"p":"“Your left lace.”"},{"p":"I looked down. It was untied."},{"p":"“Is that part of the preflight?” I asked."},{"p":"“It’s part of not tripping over your own feet before you get to the airplane.”"},{"p":"I tied it while she finished checking the lesson plan. We were scheduled for more dual instruction in the Cessna 172S, including normal approaches and landings. I’d been practicing the sight picture in my head for days. In my imagination, every approach was smooth, every correction measured, and every landing the sort that made people in the terminal look up from their coffee and applaud."},{"p":"The actual airplane had other plans."},{"p":"On our first approach, I held a little too much tension in my shoulders and let the airspeed wander."},{"p":"Mara had me correct it while maintaining the approach path. The airplane settled onto the runway firmly enough that I could feel it through the seat."},{"p":"“Not your favorite,” Mara said as we cleared the runway."},{"p":"“Was it anyone’s?”"},{"p":"“Let’s talk about why.”"},{"p":"We did. She asked what I’d noticed, what I’d missed, and how I would set up the next approach. She didn’t offer a score out of ten. I was beginning to suspect instructors trusted numbers only when they appeared on instruments."},{"p":"On the next circuit, I made smaller corrections and looked farther down the runway during the flare. Mara stayed alert, hands ready, but didn’t need to take over. The landing was noticeably smoother."},{"p":"A grin spread from my ribs to my ears."},{"p":"“There,” I said. “That’s what I meant to do.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “What made the difference?”"},{"p":"“I got stabilized earlier. Didn’t chase every little movement. Looked where I needed to look.”"},{"p":"“Good. What else?”"},{"p":"I considered it. “The wind was a little steadier?”"},{"p":"“Exactly. Your technique improved, and conditions weren’t identical. Both matter.”"},{"p":"We taxied back for another takeoff. I could feel the successful landing sitting in my chest like a warm coin. It was tempting to believe I’d crossed an invisible line. One smooth landing meant I could do another. Several meant I was nearly ready. Nearly ready meant—"},{"p":"I stopped myself."},{"p":"Mara noticed anyway. “What are you thinking?”"},{"p":"“That I liked that one.”"},{"p":"“Reasonable.”"},{"p":"“And that I want to do it again.”"},{"p":"“Also reasonable.”"},{"p":"“And that I’m trying not to turn it into proof of something bigger.”"},{"p":"She gave me a sideways look. “That’s a useful distinction.”"},{"p":"On our third approach, a mild gust changed the feel of the airplane as we descended. I made a correction, then another, a little too quickly. The runway seemed to shift in the windshield even though it was doing an excellent job of remaining exactly where it had been built."},{"p":"“Take your time with the picture,” Mara said. “Keep flying the airplane.”"},{"p":"I adjusted. For a moment I was behind it again, trying to fix the last thing instead of watching what was happening now. Mara coached me through the approach, and we landed safely, less gracefully than the one before."},{"p":"As we taxied clear, I let out a breath I hadn’t realized I was holding."},{"p":"“I thought I’d figured it out,” I said."},{"p":"“You figured out something that helped on the previous landing.”"},{"p":"“That’s not the same?”"},{"p":"“It’s part of learning. It isn’t the whole skill.”"},{"p":"I watched a small airplane roll past on the parallel taxiway. Its pilot looked entirely calm from where I sat. I wondered how many imperfect landings were hiding behind that calm face."},{"p":"Back in the briefing room, Mara opened the lesson record. She asked me to describe each approach without labeling it simply good or bad. I talked about airspeed control, timing, sight picture, corrections, and how the wind had changed. She helped me identify the things I could practice deliberately."},{"p":"Then she brought out a page headed SOLO READINESS — DISCUSSION COPY."},{"p":"My pulse jumped."},{"p":"“Discussion copy,” she repeated before I could say anything. “Not a sign-off. Not a prediction. We are reviewing what a student must eventually demonstrate.”"},{"p":"The page covered aircraft control, consistent takeoffs and landings, judgment, emergency procedures, traffic awareness, required aeronautical knowledge, and the endorsements and other requirements that would apply before a student could fly alone. Mara explained that when the time came, she would need to be satisfied I could safely perform the required maneuvers and procedures in the specific make and model, not merely that I’d had a good afternoon."},{"p":"“And you won’t be sixteen until later,” she added."},{"p":"“I have been informed by the calendar.”"},{"p":"“Then the calendar and I agree on at least one thing.”"},{"p":"She marked no boxes. Instead, she wrote three practice priorities on a separate sheet and slid it toward me."},{"p":"*Consistent approach management. Calm corrections when conditions change. Explain decisions while workload increases.*"},{"p":"I looked at the list. It was not a glamorous souvenir. There were no wings, no gold sticker, no dramatic soundtrack."},{"p":"“Am I improving?” I asked."},{"p":"“Yes,” she said without hesitation."},{"p":"The answer surprised me more than it should have."},{"p":"“Then why does it feel like the list keeps getting longer?”"},{"p":"“Because you’re learning to see more of the job.”"},{"p":"At home, Dad was fixing a kitchen drawer that had developed a talent for falling off its track. He’d placed the drawer on the floor beside a screwdriver and a pile of screws."},{"p":"“Excellent,” he said when I came in. “A second person qualified to look concerned.”"},{"p":"I knelt beside him. “What’s wrong with it?”"},{"p":"“Yesterday it opened beautifully. Today it attempted to escape.”"},{"p":"“That sounds familiar.”"},{"p":"He handed me the little plastic guide that kept the drawer aligned. One corner was cracked."},{"p":"“I thought the problem was the track,” he said. “Turns out I hadn’t checked this.”"},{"p":"I told him about the landings while he replaced the guide. He listened without pretending to understand every detail. When I got to the smooth second landing, he grinned."},{"p":"“Nice.”"},{"p":"“And the third one wasn’t as good.”"},{"p":"“Also nice.”"},{"p":"I stared at him. “You have a very broad definition.”"},{"p":"“You came home knowing what to work on. Seems useful.”"},{"p":"We slid the drawer back into place. It opened smoothly. Dad pulled it out a second time, slower, then pushed it closed again."},{"p":"“See?” he said. “One good opening isn’t a lifetime warranty.”"},{"p":"“Please don’t tell Mara you said that. She’ll put it on a poster.”"},{"p":"My phone buzzed. Rafe had sent a recording of himself practicing a simulated radio exchange with his communications mentor. In the middle, he’d accidentally called the fictional airport by the wrong name, corrected himself, and kept going."},{"p":"*I have invented a new location,* his message said. *Please update all charts.*"},{"p":"Imani sent a photograph of Nana June’s tomatoes, now tied to wooden stakes. Beckett posted a picture of his repaired bracket, which looked less crooked than before. We traded messages for fifteen minutes about whether Rafe’s imaginary airport needed a gift shop."},{"p":"Nobody asked how close I was to solo. Nobody needed to."},{"p":"Later, I set Mara’s practice sheet on my desk beside Pike’s unfinished route-verification folder. Two instructors. Two different kinds of work. Neither had promised me a date or a result."},{"p":"I thought about the smooth landing, and how quickly I’d wanted to make it mean more than it did."},{"p":"It had meant something. So had the harder one afterward."},{"p":"I opened my notebook and wrote down what I remembered while the details were still fresh. Then I added a line at the bottom:"},{"p":"*Do it again. Notice what changes.*"},{"p":"For once, that felt like enough to take into the next lesson."}]},{"t":"Chapter Five — The Person Holding the Marker","blocks":[{"p":"Captain Rowan Pike had an unusual way of making a room feel smaller. He didn’t raise his voice. He didn’t even stand at the front of the room when he spoke. He just waited until everyone noticed he was waiting, which was somehow worse."},{"p":"Our fellowship group had been assigned a table, a laminated map of an invented region, and three markers that squeaked against the plastic. Celia sat across from me with her sleeves rolled up. Beside her, a boy named Jonah was carefully arranging his pencils by length. Our fourth teammate, Anika, had arrived with a notebook full of colored tabs and the expression of someone who had already read the instructions twice."},{"p":"Pike put a folder on the table. “You’re planning a daytime training flight with an instructor. The aircraft, weather reports, and airports in this exercise are fictional. I want a route recommendation and the assumptions behind it. You have thirty minutes.”"},{"p":"I reached for the blue marker."},{"p":"Celia reached for it at exactly the same time."},{"p":"We both stopped with our fingers touching the cap."},{"p":"“Sorry,” I said, although I didn’t let go immediately."},{"p":"“You can draw,” Celia said. “I just want to look at the departure information first.”"},{"p":"“That makes sense.”"},{"p":"I released the marker. It clicked against the table. Somehow, it sounded louder than Pike’s instructions."},{"p":"Across the room, Pike moved a chair to the back wall. He wasn’t obviously watching us. The clipboard made that distinction meaningless."},{"p":"Anika spread out the weather pages. “We have a forecast that improves along the direct route, but the observation from the airport halfway there is older than the others.”"},{"p":"“Could use the southern route,” Jonah said. “It adds distance, but there are more airports.”"},{"p":"I studied the map. The direct route looked clean. Two neat legs and one obvious checkpoint near a reservoir. I could already picture how I would explain it."},{"p":"“We could start with direct,” I said. “Then check whether the reports support it.”"},{"p":"Celia tapped the top of the folder. “The instructions say choose the route after reviewing the information.”"},{"p":"“I know. I meant as a working idea.”"},{"p":"“Then call it that.”"},{"p":"Her voice wasn’t sharp. That almost made it harder to answer. She was correct in a way I didn’t particularly enjoy."},{"p":"“Working idea,” I said, writing the words at the edge of the sheet."},{"p":"For ten minutes we were a reasonably functional team. Anika compared report times. Jonah worked through the distance and time estimates using the exercise’s stated groundspeeds. Celia checked the airport information. I sketched the routes and made a list of questions we still needed to answer."},{"p":"Then Pike walked over and placed a single new page on the table."},{"p":"“Updated information,” he said."},{"p":"The simulated destination had a temporary runway closure. The alternate we’d listed was still available, but its latest weather report showed reduced visibility. We had twelve minutes left."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “Let’s switch to the southern airport.”"},{"p":"“Why?” Celia asked."},{"p":"“Because it’s open.”"},{"p":"“So is the other one, according to the packet. Open isn’t the same as suitable.”"},{"p":"I could feel my neck getting warm. “I’m trying to keep us moving.”"},{"p":"“And I’m trying to keep us from picking another airport before checking it.”"},{"p":"Jonah looked from one of us to the other. Anika kept her pencil on the weather page, waiting."},{"p":"I had been in this exact kind of disagreement before. Different room. Different map. Same uncomfortable urge to prove I was helping by deciding something quickly."},{"p":"Pike was three tables away. He hadn’t looked up."},{"p":"I put the marker down."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “I jumped ahead. Celia, what do you need checked?”"},{"p":"She took a breath. “The southern airport’s runway information and the weather trend. Jonah, can you update the time estimate? Anika, compare the timestamps? Lincoln, could you mark the options without circling a winner yet?”"},{"p":"“Absolutely,” I said. “I have extensive experience drawing circles around things prematurely.”"},{"p":"Jonah snorted. Celia’s mouth twitched, though she kept looking at the page."},{"p":"We divided the work. The revised numbers showed the southern route would be longer than our original plan, and the packet did not contain enough current information to recommend launching immediately. We agreed to present a conditional plan: delay a departure decision, obtain updated weather and airport information, then reassess the route with the instructor. The route we’d drawn was a possible option, not permission to fly."},{"p":"With two minutes remaining, Jonah discovered that one of our estimates used the wrong groundspeed. He stared at his calculation as if it had personally betrayed him."},{"p":"“I need another minute,” he said."},{"p":"“We have ninety seconds,” Celia replied."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"I looked at the clock, then at Pike. A year ago, I might have tried to talk over the problem, as though confidence could make the numbers stop existing."},{"p":"“Leave the estimate marked unverified,” I said. “We can explain what we’d recalculate.”"},{"p":"Anika drew a small box around the number and wrote CHECK before anyone could object."},{"p":"When Pike called time, our map looked less impressive than some of the others. We had three route lines, two crossed-out notes, and a large question mark beside the destination. I was not thrilled about the question mark. It was at least the size of a chicken nugget."},{"p":"Celia volunteered to present. That surprised me, mostly because I hadn’t thought to ask who wanted to do it."},{"p":"She explained the route options. Jonah described the estimate that still needed correction. Anika identified the age of the reports. I answered Pike’s question about what had changed after the new page arrived."},{"p":"“We stopped treating our first route as the answer,” I said. “We still have a plan to investigate, but we don’t have enough verified information to recommend departure.”"},{"p":"Pike regarded the map. “Who led this team?”"},{"p":"My hand almost rose. It got about an inch off my knee before I stopped it."},{"p":"“Different people at different points,” Celia said."},{"p":"“Did that work?”"},{"p":"“Eventually,” she answered."},{"p":"Pike nodded. “What slowed you down?”"},{"p":"I looked at Celia. She looked back at me, and I knew she wasn’t going to rescue me from the answer."},{"p":"“I tried to choose the route before we finished checking it,” I said. “Then I treated the questions like delays instead of part of the work.”"},{"p":"“And what changed?” Pike asked."},{"p":"“I listened.”"},{"p":"“More specifically.”"},{"p":"I had expected him to accept the nice version. Of course he hadn’t."},{"p":"“I stopped holding the marker,” I said. “We assigned jobs, identified what we didn’t know, and changed our recommendation when the information changed.”"},{"p":"Pike wrote something on his clipboard. His handwriting was too small to read from where I stood."},{"p":"After the presentations, he gave each team a short debrief. Ours included two strengths, three improvements, and no gold stars. Pike told us that a team could be polite and still fail to challenge a weak assumption. He also told us that disagreement wasn’t automatically a problem; hiding it could be."},{"p":"Celia caught up with me in the hallway."},{"p":"“You were annoyed with me,” she said."},{"p":"“A little.”"},{"p":"“I was annoyed with you.”"},{"p":"“I gathered that from your enthusiastic support.”"},{"p":"She laughed. “You did back off.”"},{"p":"“I was trying something new.”"},{"p":"“Try it again next time.”"},{"p":"“That sounds suspiciously like a compliment.”"},{"p":"“Don’t get used to it.”"},{"p":"On the ride home, Mom asked how the lab had gone. I told her about the closure, the revised route, the question mark, and Celia."},{"p":"“So you didn’t get to be in charge?” she asked."},{"p":"“I don’t think anybody was in charge the whole time.”"},{"p":"“Did that bother you?”"},{"p":"“Initially, yes. Deeply. I considered filing a complaint with the marker.”"},{"p":"Mom laughed at a red light."},{"p":"I watched a bus turn onto the road ahead of us. It was so long that the back half seemed to be traveling through a different afternoon."},{"p":"“The weird part,” I said, “is that I think we did better when I stopped trying to be the person with the answer.”"},{"p":"Mom glanced over. “You still had a job.”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“And you did it?”"},{"p":"“Eventually.”"},{"p":"She nodded as though that settled something. I wasn’t sure it settled anything, exactly. Pike’s reassessment was still ahead of me. The word *conditional* was still on my paperwork. But I had been part of a group that found a better answer than the one I had wanted to give."},{"p":"For that afternoon, it seemed like a useful thing to carry home."}]},{"t":"Chapter Six — Everybody’s Runway","blocks":[{"p":"The next Saturday, Imani invited me to help move a weather station."},{"p":"That sounded more impressive than it was. The station was a small instrument shelter mounted on a post behind the academy classroom, and the move involved a tape measure, a level, and Beckett arguing with a bag of washers."},{"p":"“It has fourteen washers,” Beckett said. “We need twelve. Why would anyone give us fourteen?”"},{"p":"“Because they know you,” I said."},{"p":"“Then they should have given us twenty.”"},{"p":"Imani stood in the grass with a notebook, checking the placement against the instructions. She’d been collecting local observations for a student project, comparing temperature readings from different spots around the academy grounds. The original location sat too close to a wall that collected afternoon heat."},{"p":"“I’m not moving it just because the numbers look weird,” she said. “I’m moving it because the site doesn’t meet the placement guidance for what I’m trying to measure.”"},{"p":"“That is a very Imani sentence,” I said."},{"p":"“Thank you.”"},{"p":"“It wasn’t a criticism.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"A year ago, I might have filled the quiet with questions about her grandmother. I had learned that being invited to help with a weather station was an invitation to help with a weather station. If Imani wanted to tell me something else, she could."},{"p":"We set the post and checked the level twice. Beckett tightened the bracket, stepped back, and frowned."},{"p":"“It’s crooked.”"},{"p":"“It is not,” Imani said."},{"p":"“It’s a tiny bit crooked.”"},{"p":"I leaned sideways. “If I tilt my head, it’s perfect.”"},{"p":"“Your head isn’t a calibrated instrument,” Beckett told me."},{"p":"He adjusted the bracket. When the bubble centered, he looked satisfied for three seconds before noticing the extra washers again."},{"p":"Imani took the first reading and wrote the time beside it. Then she looked at me."},{"p":"“How’s the landing practice?”"},{"p":"“Some landings are better than others.”"},{"p":"“That’s a brave statement from someone who once described a single good landing as ‘basically the future.’”"},{"p":"“I have matured.”"},{"p":"“Since Tuesday?”"},{"p":"“Rapidly.”"},{"p":"She smiled and returned to her notebook."},{"p":"A little later, I walked with Beckett to the workshop where he was testing a model control linkage. He’d built a simple demonstration rig with hinges and rods, meant to show younger students how moving a cockpit control could change a control surface’s position. It was not an aircraft component, which Beckett emphasized so often that I suspected someone had once asked to install it on an airplane."},{"p":"He moved the lever. A cardboard elevator rose smoothly, then stuck halfway down."},{"p":"“That’s the problem,” he said. “Sometimes it binds.”"},{"p":"“Have you tried telling it to be more consistent?”"},{"p":"“Several times. It’s unresponsive to leadership.”"},{"p":"We traced the motion and found that one connecting rod was rubbing against the edge of a support. Beckett loosened the mount, adjusted the spacing, and tried again."},{"p":"The cardboard moved freely."},{"p":"“Beautiful,” I said."},{"p":"“Once,” he replied."},{"p":"He worked the lever twenty more times, watching for the snag to return. I recognized the expression on his face. It was the same one Mara wore when she asked me to repeat a maneuver instead of celebrating the first decent attempt."},{"p":"“You’ve become my flight instructor,” I told him."},{"p":"“Good. My rates are lower.”"},{"p":"“Do you accept pretzels?”"},{"p":"“I accept correct hardware.”"},{"p":"Rafe wasn’t at the academy that morning. He was visiting a volunteer-run aviation communications demonstration at a community event, where his mentor had agreed to let him help explain how pilots and controllers use clear, concise radio calls. He was not directing real aircraft. Rafe had made that distinction in our group chat with seven exclamation points, followed by a photograph of himself wearing a headset that appeared to be twice the size of his skull."},{"p":"At noon he called us from a picnic table outside the event."},{"p":"“I successfully explained the phonetic alphabet to six children and one confused grandfather,” he announced."},{"p":"“Did you invent another airport?” I asked."},{"p":"“Only two. Neither is currently accepting arrivals.”"},{"p":"Then he got serious for a moment. “My mentor says I can help prepare the next demonstration. Actual planning. Like figuring out what people need to learn and what examples make sense.”"},{"p":"“That sounds perfect for you,” I said."},{"p":"There was a pause, just long enough that I wondered whether the connection had dropped."},{"p":"“Thanks,” he said. “I think it might be.”"},{"p":"After we hung up, Beckett went back to his linkage and Imani recorded another observation. Nobody’s project looked like mine. Nobody’s project was supposed to."},{"p":"My own lesson with Mara began after lunch. We reviewed the weather, the aircraft, and the objectives for the flight. Mara made the decisions that belonged to the instructor and asked me to explain what I was seeing. The day was suitable for the planned training, with light winds that had enough variation to make me pay attention."},{"p":"We practiced in the traffic pattern. On the first approach, I let the airplane drift away from the alignment I wanted and corrected too late. Mara coached me through a safe landing, then asked what I had noticed."},{"p":"“The drift started earlier than I acted on it,” I said."},{"p":"“Right. What will you watch next time?”"},{"p":"“Runway alignment, airspeed, and whether I’m staying ahead of the corrections.”"},{"p":"“Good. Don’t chase a perfect-looking landing. Fly a stable approach, and be prepared to go around if it isn’t working.”"},{"p":"On the second circuit, I was so determined to be smooth that I hesitated when I needed a small correction. I fixed that, then became too busy congratulating myself for fixing it. The airplane kept moving anyway."},{"p":"“Keep your attention outside,” Mara reminded me."},{"p":"I did. We landed safely. Fine, not beautiful. I was starting to appreciate that category."},{"p":"On the third approach, the wind shifted a little. I made one correction, checked the result, and resisted the urge to make three more just because my hands were available. The airplane settled onto the runway without drama."},{"p":"My face tried to turn into a grin. I kept it mostly under control."},{"p":"“Tell me about that one,” Mara said after we cleared the runway."},{"p":"“I noticed the drift earlier. I made a smaller correction. And I didn’t try to repair something that had already improved.”"},{"p":"“That’s a better description than ‘nailed it.’”"},{"p":"“I had prepared a speech.”"},{"p":"“You can save it.”"},{"p":"Back in the classroom, Mara wrote her notes while I filled out my own. Three approaches. Three different lessons. The last one had gone well. I resisted turning it into a verdict on my future."},{"p":"“Can I ask something?” I said."},{"p":"“You usually do.”"},{"p":"“Does Pike’s reassessment affect whether I can eventually solo?”"},{"p":"Mara closed her pen. “His fellowship evaluation and your flight-training endorsements are separate things. But the habits he’s asking you to demonstrate—checking information, making sound decisions, accepting correction— matter in both places. I won’t treat a fellowship result as a substitute for my own assessment of your flying.”"},{"p":"“So I can’t pass one and automatically pass the other.”"},{"p":"“Correct.”"},{"p":"“And failing one doesn’t automatically decide the other.”"},{"p":"“Also correct. We look at what you can consistently do, and what the requirements are.”"},{"p":"I considered that. It would have been easier if there were one enormous test at the end of everything. One test, one score, one triumphant musical number. Instead, there were separate skills, separate responsibilities, and people who expected me to demonstrate them more than once."},{"p":"When I got home, Dad was sitting at the kitchen table with a library book and a birthday card he’d bought too early. The envelope had my name on it, written in his unmistakable handwriting."},{"p":"“You’re not supposed to see that,” he said."},{"p":"“Then why is it in the middle of the table?”"},{"p":"“Terrible security procedures.”"},{"p":"He slid the card under the book. I could still see one corner."},{"p":"“Sixteen is coming,” he said."},{"p":"“I’m aware.”"},{"p":"“You excited?”"},{"p":"“Very.”"},{"p":"“Nervous?”"},{"p":"I thought about Pike’s clipboard, Mara’s notes, and the three landings that had each felt different. I thought about Imani’s careful weather readings, Beckett testing his model over and over, and Rafe finding a place where his voice mattered."},{"p":"“Also very,” I said."},{"p":"Dad nodded. “Sounds about right.”"},{"p":"My phone lit up with a new message from Pike’s fellowship office. It wasn’t the reassessment appointment I’d been expecting. It was a notice that our next group session would include peer review: each student would have to explain another student’s plan fairly before offering criticism."},{"p":"I read it twice."},{"p":"“Good news?” Dad asked."},{"p":"“I think so.”"},{"p":"“You don’t sound sure.”"},{"p":"“I’m trying not to decide before I check.”"},{"p":"He smiled and pushed the hidden birthday card farther beneath his book."},{"p":"For once, I didn’t ask to see it."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seven — Somebody Else’s Map","blocks":[{"p":"Captain Pike had arranged the tables in a square, which was never a good sign."},{"p":"In the normal fellowship sessions, the tables formed islands. You could huddle with your team, slide papers around, and make the occasional quiet comment about how a ruler was clearly designed by someone who hated straight lines. A square meant we could all see one another. It also meant Pike could see exactly who was pretending to read."},{"p":"He stood beside a whiteboard with his sleeves rolled up. Four folders waited on the front table."},{"p":"“Today’s assignment is not to improve a flight plan,” he said. “At least, not at first.”"},{"p":"Celia raised her hand. “Are we allowed to ask what the assignment is before we start?”"},{"p":"“You just did.”"},{"p":"“Excellent. I enjoy making progress.”"},{"p":"Pike’s mouth almost moved into a smile. “Each of you will receive a plan prepared by someone else. You will explain the reasoning behind it as accurately and fairly as you can. The author will tell us whether you represented it correctly. Only then may you evaluate it.”"},{"p":"I looked at Celia. She looked at me. We both looked away with the speed of people who had just realized they’d been assigned a group project by the universe."},{"p":"Pike handed me her folder."},{"p":"Naturally."},{"p":"Her fictional route ran between two small airports, with a planned stop at an intermediate field. She had chosen a slightly longer track than I would have. Her notes listed two reasons: a better-defined sequence of visual checkpoints and an alternate airport she considered more suitable if the forecast winds became less favorable."},{"p":"I found a pencil mark beside the second checkpoint and immediately wanted to argue. The landmark was a highway interchange, and there was another one that looked similar farther east. Why use something so easy to confuse?"},{"p":"I put my pencil down."},{"p":"The assignment wasn’t to argue yet."},{"p":"Across from me, Celia was reading my plan with the expression of someone examining a sandwich that might contain a live insect."},{"p":"We had twenty minutes to prepare. I read her route again. Then I traced the checkpoints in order, trying to understand what problem she had been solving before hunting for mistakes. The longer track kept the route nearer several distinctive features. The intermediate field had services the other candidate lacked in our exercise. She had included a note that the airport’s published information would need to be checked again before an actual flight."},{"p":"I was still uncertain about the interchange. But I could see why she’d made the choice."},{"p":"When Pike called us together, I went first."},{"p":"“Celia planned the longer route because she wanted checkpoints that would be easier to verify as a sequence,” I said. “The stop isn’t just the closest airport. She considered the airport information, forecast conditions, and what options would remain if things changed.”"},{"p":"I turned to her. “You also marked information that needs another check before departure.”"},{"p":"Celia nodded slowly. “That’s what I meant.”"},{"p":"Four words. After the way we’d started the fellowship, they felt like successfully docking a spaceship."},{"p":"“Now,” Pike said, “you may evaluate.”"},{"p":"“I’d want to look more closely at the interchange checkpoint,” I said. “There are two similar ones. I might choose another nearby feature or make sure I had a second reference.”"},{"p":"“Fair,” Celia said. “I noticed that after I finished, but I didn’t change it because I wanted to ask the group.”"},{"p":"“Why didn’t you write that?” I asked."},{"p":"She tapped the edge of her folder. “I ran out of room.”"},{"p":"For a moment we stared at the same little pencil mark."},{"p":"Then we both laughed."},{"p":"Pike made a note. “What happened there?”"},{"p":"“I understood her reasoning before I challenged it,” I said."},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"“She was already aware of the weakness I thought I’d discovered.”"},{"p":"Celia leaned back. “Also, I apparently need larger paper.”"},{"p":"When it was her turn to explain my route, she did it well. She described the reasons for my checkpoints and the conditions under which I’d reconsider the destination. She even explained my fuel-planning notes without rolling her eyes. Tremendous personal growth."},{"p":"Then she pointed to a place where I’d written “confirm before departure.”"},{"p":"“You have three items with that note,” she said. “I understand why they’re open. But how will you make sure you actually close them?”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth and nearly said, I’ll remember."},{"p":"The last time I’d trusted that answer, Captain Pike had used a red pencil."},{"p":"“I need a way to track them,” I said instead. “Not just leave them on the page.”"},{"p":"Pike nodded once. “A plan can acknowledge uncertainty. It must also tell you what to do about it.”"},{"p":"We spent the rest of the session revising our fictional plans. No one won. Pike didn’t award points for being the first to find an error. By the end, we had a better route, clearer notes, and an actual checklist for information that needed updating."},{"p":"When we packed up, Celia slid my folder across the table."},{"p":"“You’re less annoying when you listen,” she said."},{"p":"“That’s going on my college applications.”"},{"p":"“I’ll write a recommendation.”"},{"p":"Outside, I called Dad from the walkway. He was at the library, where someone had apparently returned a book with a slice of cheese between the pages."},{"p":"“Was it a cookbook?” I asked."},{"p":"“No. Ancient civilizations.”"},{"p":"“Maybe the cheese was an artifact.”"},{"p":"“It’s in the trash, which is where artifacts go when they smell like that.”"},{"p":"I told him about the exercise while he walked toward his car."},{"p":"“So you and Celia agreed?” he said."},{"p":"“Eventually. We also disagreed, but in a useful way.”"},{"p":"“Dangerous. Next you’ll be organizing your room.”"},{"p":"“Let’s not say things we can’t take back.”"},{"p":"That evening I opened the route-verification notebook I’d started after Pike’s conditional evaluation. I added a page titled OPEN ITEMS. Under it, I drew three columns: question, source to check, and status. I didn’t decorate it. Beckett would have called that a missed opportunity."},{"p":"I stared at the empty columns for a while."},{"p":"I had spent months thinking a strong plan was one without questions. Now I was beginning to see the value of knowing which questions were still open."},{"p":"I put the notebook beside my flight bag so I wouldn’t forget it in the morning."},{"p":"Then I went downstairs to help Dad make dinner, where I discovered that the recipe also had an open item."},{"p":"“Does ‘one clove of garlic’ mean one little piece,” he asked, “or the entire thing?”"},{"p":"“Dad.”"},{"p":"“I just want to be thorough.”"},{"p":"I found the garlic before he could turn spaghetti night into a medical emergency."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eight — Sixteen Candles, No Shortcuts","blocks":[{"p":"On the morning of my sixteenth birthday, I woke to the sound of a lawn mower and the conviction that something enormous was supposed to happen."},{"p":"I sat up and waited."},{"p":"Nothing happened."},{"p":"My bedroom looked the same. The model airplane above my desk had not become a real one. My phone had eleven messages, three of which were from Rafe and one of which was a photograph of a traffic cone wearing sunglasses."},{"p":"Rafe’s caption read: CONGRATULATIONS. YOU ARE NOW OLD ENOUGH TO BE TRUSTED WITH THE CONE."},{"p":"I wrote back: Is it airworthy?"},{"p":"He replied: NOT WITH THAT ATTITUDE."},{"p":"Downstairs, Mom had made pancakes shaped like airplanes. The wings were uneven and one appeared to have suffered a structural failure during its encounter with the spatula."},{"p":"“Experimental design,” she said when I examined it."},{"p":"Dad placed a tiny paper boarding pass beside my plate. It read: PASSENGER LINCOLN BURGESS. DESTINATION: SIXTEEN. BOARDING TIME: WHENEVER YOU FINISH BREAKFAST."},{"p":"“You know that’s not how boarding passes work,” I said."},{"p":"“You’re welcome,” he said."},{"p":"Uncle Everett arrived carrying a narrow gift bag and the careful expression he wore when he was trying not to give away a joke. Inside was a sturdy notebook with a plain blue cover. On the first page he’d written: WRITE DOWN WHAT YOU LEARN. ESPECIALLY THE PARTS YOU’D RATHER FORGET."},{"p":"“That’s not a very festive birthday message,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s cheaper than learning the same lesson twice.”"},{"p":"Mom handed me a second envelope. Inside was a gift certificate for a visit to the EAA Aviation Museum in Oshkosh, Wisconsin, later in the year, when our family could arrange the travel."},{"p":"I read it again."},{"p":"“Wisconsin?”"},{"p":"“Actual Wisconsin,” Dad said. “Not the cardboard version Beckett would build in the garage.”"},{"p":"I’d seen photographs of the museum, aircraft suspended above visitors, rows of machines that had crossed continents or helped people learn to fly. I knew the trip would take planning and money. I had heard Mom and Dad discussing both after they thought I was asleep."},{"p":"“Are you sure?” I asked."},{"p":"“We’ve budgeted for it,” Mom said. “We’ll work out the dates together.”"},{"p":"I hugged her, then Dad, then Uncle Everett, who made a noise suggesting his ribs had filed a complaint."},{"p":"The party wasn’t enormous. That afternoon, Imani came by with a small weather notebook she’d made from a stack of observation sheets. Beckett brought a wooden model of a control yoke that moved more smoothly than the one he’d shown me last month. Rafe arrived with a homemade certificate proclaiming me MOST LIKELY TO READ THE ENTIRE CHECKLIST OUT LOUD AT A RESTAURANT."},{"p":"“That’s a compliment,” he assured me."},{"p":"Imani stood beside the kitchen counter while Dad cut cake. Her braids were tied back, and she’d brought a photograph of Nana June’s tomato plant, now tall enough to need another stake."},{"p":"“She says you can come help with the next one,” Imani told me."},{"p":"“Does she know I nearly killed the first one?”"},{"p":"“She says you only watered the sidewalk twice.”"},{"p":"“That’s improvement.”"},{"p":"Imani smiled. We stood there without filling every silence. It was easier than it had been months ago. She still chose what to share about her grandmother, and I didn’t ask for more. I was learning to be worthy of that trust."},{"p":"Later, when the others had gone, I sat on the porch steps with Uncle Everett. A warm evening breeze moved through the trees, carrying the smell of cut grass."},{"p":"“So,” he said, “sixteen.”"},{"p":"“Apparently.”"},{"p":"“Feeling different?”"},{"p":"“I thought I would.”"},{"p":"“Give it time.”"},{"p":"I picked at the corner of my new notebook. “It’s the minimum age for soloing an airplane. Everyone keeps saying that, and I know it doesn’t mean I’m ready. But it’s hard not to feel like a clock started today.”"},{"p":"He looked toward the street. “A clock can tell you when you meet an age requirement. It can’t tell you how well you’ll handle a landing.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Do you?”"},{"p":"I sighed. “I’m working on it.”"},{"p":"“That’s an answer I believe.”"},{"p":"He didn’t tell me about the first time he’d flown alone. I was grateful. Not because I didn’t want the story, but because I didn’t want to compare myself to it tonight."},{"p":"At my next lesson, Mara had already reviewed the weather and the training plan. We discussed what I’d done well recently and what still needed work. My birthday earned a warm congratulations and exactly zero shortcuts."},{"p":"“Today is dual instruction,” she said. “We’ll work on stabilized approaches, recognizing when a go-around is appropriate, and making corrections without overcontrolling.”"},{"p":"“I was hoping the airplane would sense that I’m sixteen and cooperate.”"},{"p":"“Airplanes are notoriously indifferent to birthdays.”"},{"p":"The first circuit went well enough that I almost began writing a victory speech in my head. On the next approach, I let the picture get away from me. The airplane wasn’t lined up the way I wanted, and I caught myself trying to fix too many things at once."},{"p":"“Look at the whole approach,” Mara said. “What are your options?”"},{"p":"I looked, assessed, and said, “Go around.”"},{"p":"“Agreed. I’ll supervise. Fly the procedure we’ve practiced.”"},{"p":"We discontinued the approach and climbed away, then returned to the pattern after a reset. There was no dramatic music. Nobody gasped. It was simply the safe decision for the situation, made early enough that it stayed ordinary."},{"p":"On the next approach I kept my attention outside, made smaller corrections, and maintained a steadier picture. Mara remained ready to intervene, as she always did. We landed without fuss."},{"p":"After securing the airplane, she asked me to compare the approaches."},{"p":"“The first was fine,” I said. “On the second I tried to save an approach that wasn’t settling down. On the third I didn’t rush the corrections.”"},{"p":"“And the go-around?”"},{"p":"“I wish I hadn’t needed it.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because I wanted to prove I was getting consistent.”"},{"p":"She folded her arms. “What did you prove instead?”"},{"p":"I considered the question. “That I could recognize a problem and stop trying to force the landing.”"},{"p":"“That’s worth learning. We still need consistency, and we’ll keep practicing.”"},{"p":"In the office, Mara showed me the broad areas we’d continue reviewing before any solo authorization: applicable aeronautical knowledge, the aircraft and local procedures, required maneuvers, emergency responses, judgment, and repeated demonstrations of proficiency. She explained that the required pre-solo knowledge test and endorsements would be handled when my training justified them, not because the calendar had changed."},{"p":"I wrote the areas in my notebook. There was plenty of space left beneath them."},{"p":"On the drive home, Mom asked how it had gone."},{"p":"“I went around once,” I said."},{"p":"“Is that bad?”"},{"p":"“No. It was the right call.”"},{"p":"“Then why do you sound disappointed?”"},{"p":"I watched a row of trees slide past the window. “Because I wanted today to feel like a big step.”"},{"p":"Mom reached over at a red light and squeezed my shoulder."},{"p":"“Sometimes the big step is making the decision you didn’t want to need.”"},{"p":"At home, I opened Uncle Everett’s notebook to its first blank page. I wrote the date and, underneath it, one sentence:"},{"p":"TURNING SIXTEEN CHANGED WHAT WAS POSSIBLE. IT DIDN’T CHANGE WHAT I HAD TO LEARN."},{"p":"Then I crossed out the first word. Not because it was wrong, but because I wanted the sentence to sound like me."},{"p":"I wrote it again beneath the crossed-out line."},{"p":"SIXTEEN MEANS I CAN WORK TOWARD SOLO. IT DOESN’T MEAN THE AIRPLANE OWES ME ONE."},{"p":"That was better."},{"p":"From downstairs, Dad called, “Lincoln! Do you know where the birthday candles went?”"},{"p":"I found them in the refrigerator beside the leftover cake."},{"p":"He looked at the candles, then at me. “I have no explanation.”"},{"p":"“It’s okay,” I said. “We can open an investigation.”"},{"p":"“Please don’t invite Captain Pike.”"},{"p":"I laughed hard enough that Mom came in to see what had happened. Nothing extraordinary had happened, which was exactly what I needed."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nine — The Question Behind the Question","blocks":[{"p":"The trouble with a birthday is that it happens whether you are ready for it or not."},{"p":"Three days after turning sixteen, I was sitting in a folding chair at the fellowship lab, looking at a paper airplane somebody had wedged into the ceiling tiles. Its left wing had folded in on itself. It had been there since my first visit, and every week it seemed to sag another fraction of an inch."},{"p":"I understood the feeling."},{"p":"Captain Pike stood beside a whiteboard on which he’d written two words: YOUR RECOMMENDATION."},{"p":"“Today’s exercise,” he said, “will end with a decision. Not a perfect decision. Not a speech. A decision your group can defend using the information you actually have.”"},{"p":"Celia, sitting across from me, flipped open her notebook. “Do we get to know the scenario before we start defending it?”"},{"p":"“You may find that helpful,” Pike said."},{"p":"The fellowship had rearranged our groups for the next block. Marcus and Ellis had moved to another scenario table, while Nico was helping Ms. Alvarez test the communications exercise down the hall. Celia and I stayed together. Our new teammates were Nora Patel, who had a habit of checking numbers twice before trusting them, and Dev Mercer, who kept a pocket notebook full of airport questions. We had met them at the previous lab, but this was our first exercise together."},{"p":"Nora and Dev laughed. I was still learning how to tell when Pike was making a joke. His expression didn’t change much, which made him either a very efficient instructor or the world’s least successful comedian."},{"p":"He handed us a fictional route packet: three possible destinations, a forecast that might or might not improve, airport information, and a message from a pretend passenger who absolutely had to arrive before lunch."},{"p":"“The passenger has a meeting,” Nora read. “Apparently a very important meeting.”"},{"p":"“Every meeting is important to the person who scheduled it,” Dev said."},{"p":"“Not my dentist appointments,” I said."},{"p":"Pike looked over. “Mr. Burgess. Your dentist is not one of the available alternates.”"},{"p":"I shut my mouth."},{"p":"We divided the work. Celia examined the destination weather and its timing. Dev checked the airport information. Nora calculated rough travel times using the groundspeeds provided in the exercise. I worked through the route and restrictions, using the verification checklist I’d been rebuilding since Pike’s red-pencil evaluation."},{"p":"At first, the pieces fit. The middle destination had the shortest estimated travel time and an acceptable-looking forecast for the proposed arrival period. I could almost hear my own voice saying, *Option B. Obviously.*"},{"p":"Then Celia tapped the timestamp on the forecast."},{"p":"“Wait. This was issued earlier than the update in the supplemental packet.”"},{"p":"She pulled out a second page. The newer information suggested a lower cloud ceiling during the relevant window. It didn’t automatically rule out every possible flight, but it made the easy answer much less easy."},{"p":"“Could we just move the departure?” Nora asked."},{"p":"“Maybe,” Dev said. “But the airport’s services are limited later in the day, and the packet doesn’t say whether they’re available.”"},{"p":"I looked at my checklist. Route restrictions. Current information. Airport suitability. Alternatives."},{"p":"Questions that still needed answers."},{"p":"“We should recommend Option C,” I said. “Longer, but the weather picture is more favorable.”"},{"p":"Celia leaned forward. “I agree C looks better on weather. But have you checked its arrival-time estimate against the fuel assumptions?”"},{"p":"“I checked the route.”"},{"p":"“That’s not what I asked.”"},{"p":"My neck warmed. I could have said that I was getting to it. I could have reminded her that we’d split the work and Nora had the time estimates. I could have made a joke about dentists."},{"p":"Instead I looked at Nora. “Can you walk us through what you found?”"},{"p":"She did. Option C was longer than I’d assumed. The exercise included a headwind, and the extra time mattered. We weren’t being asked to calculate a legal fuel reserve from scratch; we were being asked to recognize that the planning assumptions and the supplied fuel figures needed to agree. They didn’t yet."},{"p":"“Then we don’t have a finished recommendation,” I said."},{"p":"“Good,” Celia replied. “Now we’re getting somewhere.”"},{"p":"It sounded insulting until I noticed she was smiling."},{"p":"We built a conditional answer: do not launch on the information currently supplied; obtain an updated briefing, verify airport suitability and fuel planning, and reconsider the options only when those gaps were closed. If the necessary conditions couldn’t be met, the fictional trip would wait. The passenger’s meeting didn’t get a vote on the weather."},{"p":"When Pike called time, Dev presented our conclusion. He had a soft voice, so I watched him keep his eyes on his notes while he started. Halfway through, he looked up. By the end he sounded like someone who believed the words he was saying."},{"p":"Pike listened without interrupting."},{"p":"“Who chose not to recommend departure?” he asked."},{"p":"“All of us,” Dev said."},{"p":"“Who identified the issue that changed the group’s direction?”"},{"p":"“Celia,” I said."},{"p":"“And who found the problem with the alternate?”"},{"p":"“Nora.”"},{"p":"Pike turned to me. “What was your contribution?”"},{"p":"I looked at my checklist, crowded with boxes, arrows, and one coffee stain I couldn’t explain."},{"p":"“I helped make sure we checked the whole plan,” I said. “And I stopped trying to pick an answer before we’d heard everyone.”"},{"p":"Pike nodded once. “Those are two different contributions. Both useful.”"},{"p":"For a moment I wanted him to say more. Something about how much I’d improved, maybe. Something about the reassessment."},{"p":"Instead he asked Dev to explain the group’s remaining unknowns."},{"p":"After the lab, I caught up with Celia by the vending machines. She was studying a bag of pretzels as if it had personally offended her."},{"p":"“You were right about the fuel assumption,” I said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Wow. You could at least pretend to be humble.”"},{"p":"She bought the pretzels. “You were right about not making a recommendation until we had the rest of the information.”"},{"p":"“I know,” I said."},{"p":"“See? Humility is exhausting.”"},{"p":"We walked back toward the classroom. The paper airplane still hung above us, one wing lower than the other."},{"p":"“Do you think Pike’s ever going to let me retake the route-verification evaluation?” I asked."},{"p":"Celia considered that. “He keeps giving you chances to show him how you think.”"},{"p":"“That’s not the same as passing.”"},{"p":"“No,” she said. “But it’s probably not nothing.”"},{"p":"When I got home, Dad was fixing the kitchen drawer that had been sticking for months. He had all the utensils spread across the counter, including a suspicious number of bottle openers."},{"p":"“How was fellowship?” he asked."},{"p":"“We recommended not flying.”"},{"p":"“Sounds economical.”"},{"p":"“I’m starting to think my entire aviation career will consist of explaining why imaginary airplanes should remain on the ground.”"},{"p":"He held up a bent screw. “At least your imaginary airplanes don’t require hardware.”"},{"p":"I helped him put the drawer back together. The first time we tried it, it jammed. The second time, we realized a utensil tray was sitting crooked. We fixed it and tried again."},{"p":"The drawer slid shut so smoothly Dad opened it twice more, just to admire it."},{"p":"“Look at that,” he said. “We are practically engineers.”"},{"p":"“Let’s not tell Beckett.”"},{"p":"That night I opened my navigation folder and looked at the old red-pencil mark beside my missed route restriction. I didn’t erase it. I added a new page behind it, one that began with a question instead of a box."},{"p":"*What would make this recommendation wrong?*"},{"p":"It wasn’t the sort of question I enjoyed asking."},{"p":"Which was probably why I needed it."}]},{"t":"Chapter Ten — The Landing That Didn’t Count","blocks":[{"p":"On Saturday morning, Beckett texted me a picture of a model airplane sitting on his workbench. Its nose was missing."},{"p":"**BECKETT:** Prototype has encountered a design disagreement."},{"p":"**ME:** With what?"},{"p":"**BECKETT:** Gravity."},{"p":"A minute later another picture arrived. This time the nose was attached with a rubber band and what looked like enough tape to secure a small bridge."},{"p":"**BECKETT:** Gravity and I are negotiating."},{"p":"I was still laughing when Mom dropped me at Hampton Roads Executive Airport."},{"p":"Mara was at the desk, reviewing the weather and our lesson plan. The wind was within the conditions she’d selected for training, though it wasn’t perfectly still. We would be practicing normal approaches and landings, with attention to maintaining a stable approach, making measured corrections, and recognizing when to go around. Nothing in the plan involved me flying alone."},{"p":"“What’s funny?” she asked."},{"p":"I showed her the picture."},{"p":"“Tell Beckett to keep the rubber bands away from actual airplanes,” she said."},{"p":"“He would like that printed on a certificate.”"},{"p":"We went through our normal preflight routine. I inspected the airplane with Mara’s oversight, discussed the day’s conditions and procedures, and made sure I understood the lesson’s priorities. My sixteenth birthday was already old news to the Cessna."},{"p":"In the cockpit, I adjusted my seat and harness, then glanced at the empty passenger seats behind us. I’d been imagining this airplane without Mara beside me so often that the thought had begun to feel familiar."},{"p":"But familiar wasn’t the same as real."},{"p":"We taxied, completed the required checks, and took off under Mara’s supervision. In the pattern, the first approach was ordinary. I maintained a reasonably steady picture, responded to small changes without chasing every movement, and landed with Mara coaching only when needed."},{"p":"“Good,” she said as we continued. “What helped?”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t trying to make the airplane do everything at once.”"},{"p":"“Keep that thought.”"},{"p":"The second approach was better. Not movie-perfect. Not the kind where a crowd applauded from the terminal. Just better. My hands were quieter, and I had more attention left for what was happening around us."},{"p":"On the third, I made a correction a little late. Then another. I could feel myself getting busy, the way I did when a math problem looked simple until the numbers stopped behaving."},{"p":"“Assess the approach,” Mara said."},{"p":"I checked the overall picture. It was still manageable at that moment, but my attention had narrowed. I was working hard to preserve a landing that didn’t need preserving."},{"p":"“Go around,” I said."},{"p":"“Agreed.”"},{"p":"We followed the practiced go-around procedure, with Mara supervising, and returned for another circuit."},{"p":"I wanted to be proud of choosing correctly. I also wanted the third approach to have been as smooth as the second. Those feelings argued with each other all the way around the pattern."},{"p":"The next landing was decent. I knew it before Mara said anything, which was part of the problem. The instant I thought *There it is*, I wondered whether Mara agreed."},{"p":"After shutdown, she took her time putting away the paperwork. I took my time pretending not to wait for a verdict."},{"p":"“Tell me about the lesson,” she said."},{"p":"“Two good landings. One that got messy. A go-around. Then another good landing.”"},{"p":"“Which was your best decision?”"},{"p":"“The go-around.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because I recognized I was getting behind the airplane and stopped trying to force the result.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “What would you like to improve?”"},{"p":"“Doing that sooner. And keeping my corrections smaller before it gets to that point.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"I waited."},{"p":"Mara looked at me. “There’s another question you want to ask.”"},{"p":"I sighed. “Am I getting closer?”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"The word stayed with me."},{"p":"“Does that mean—”"},{"p":"“It means you’re improving. We still need to see consistent proficiency across the required areas, including the applicable knowledge and emergency procedures. Before any solo, there are specific training and endorsement requirements, and I’ll decide when you’re ready to meet them. I won’t guess a date.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“You’re allowed to be excited, Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“I’m also allowed to be impatient, right?”"},{"p":"“Certainly. You just aren’t allowed to let impatience fly the airplane.”"},{"p":"At home, Uncle Everett was helping Dad replace a porch light. I stood at the bottom of the ladder and told them about the lesson."},{"p":"“Three landings and a go-around,” I said."},{"p":"“Four approaches that taught you something,” Uncle Everett corrected."},{"p":"“Do retired Navy pilots have to turn everything into a lesson?”"},{"p":"“Only the interesting things.”"},{"p":"Dad, holding a screwdriver, said, “Ask him about grocery shopping. He’ll give you a debrief.”"},{"p":"Everett ignored him. “You sound disappointed about the go-around.”"},{"p":"“Not disappointed exactly. I know it was right. I just keep wondering when all the pieces are going to stay together.”"},{"p":"He climbed down from the ladder before answering. “Some days they’ll come together better than others. Training is partly learning the skills, and partly learning to notice when the situation isn’t what you expected.”"},{"p":"I looked at the porch light. “So I’m supposed to feel good about the landing I didn’t make?”"},{"p":"“You don’t have to feel good about it. You do have to understand why you chose not to make it.”"},{"p":"That sounded suspiciously like Mara. I accused them of holding secret instructor meetings."},{"p":"“It’s on Thursdays,” Dad said. “We serve terrible coffee.”"},{"p":"That evening I met Rafe at the public library. He had spread a stack of index cards across a table, each covered in neat handwriting and a small drawing of a radio."},{"p":"“I’m helping with a communications demonstration next month,” he said. “The club wants a beginner-friendly segment about why pilots listen before they transmit.”"},{"p":"“Good plan,” I said. “Especially for you.”"},{"p":"He tossed an index card at my head."},{"p":"I caught it. On the back he’d written: **SPEAK CLEARLY. LISTEN COMPLETELY. DON’T GUESS.**"},{"p":"“Those are pretty good,” I said."},{"p":"“Thank you. I’m becoming a responsible adult.”"},{"p":"“You’re sixteen.”"},{"p":"“Responsible adult in training.”"},{"p":"We worked on the cards for half an hour. Rafe practiced explaining how radio communication helps pilots share information and follow instructions without pretending every frequency worked the same way. When he got too technical, I raised a hand and asked the kind of question a younger kid might ask. When I offered a terrible joke, he rejected it with the authority of a man running a very small radio empire."},{"p":"At one point he stopped and looked at me. “How’s the solo thing?”"},{"p":"“Still not a thing.”"},{"p":"“Yet.”"},{"p":"“Yet,” I agreed."},{"p":"He straightened a crooked index card. “I used to think that if you got ahead of me, it meant I was falling behind.”"},{"p":"I remembered the empty chair at the fellowship lab, and the argument we’d had because neither of us knew what to do with his disappointment."},{"p":"“I used to think everyone was measuring the same thing,” I said."},{"p":"“Turns out we’re not.”"},{"p":"“Good. Because you’d beat me at talking.”"},{"p":"“And you’d beat me at drawing airports that don’t exist.”"},{"p":"We grinned at each other. For once, neither of us needed to explain the joke."},{"p":"Before bed, I wrote two entries in my notebook."},{"p":"**Things I did well:** recognized an unstable trend, chose a go-around, returned to a calmer approach."},{"p":"**Things I still need:** more consistent corrections, broader attention, more practice."},{"p":"I almost added *one step closer to solo*."},{"p":"Then I stopped. It wasn’t wrong, exactly. It just wasn’t the most useful thing I could write."},{"p":"So I added a third line instead."},{"p":"**A landing isn’t the only way to finish an approach well.**"},{"p":"Downstairs, Dad switched on the repaired porch light. Through my window I watched the bright square fall across the front steps."},{"p":"It stayed on."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eleven — The Page with No Checkmarks","blocks":[{"p":"Captain Pike’s reassessment began with an empty sheet of paper."},{"p":"That was the first bad sign."},{"p":"The second was that he left me standing."},{"p":"He stood beside the long table in the fellowship classroom, sleeves rolled neatly to his forearms, looking at the blank page as if it had done something to disappoint him."},{"p":"“Your route-verification worksheet,” he said."},{"p":"“I brought the revised version.” I lifted my folder."},{"p":"“I know. I read it. Put it away.”"},{"p":"I lowered the folder."},{"p":"Across the room, the wall clock made the sort of loud, deliberate ticking that clocks only make when somebody is being evaluated."},{"p":"“Today’s scenario is unfamiliar,” Pike said. “You’ll have the materials you need to evaluate it, but not necessarily every answer you might want. Explain your decisions. Ask questions. And if the information doesn’t support a conclusion, say so.”"},{"p":"“Yes, sir.”"},{"p":"He slid a packet toward me. At the top, in bold letters, someone had printed TRAINING SCENARIO — NOT FOR FLIGHT USE. Below it was a fictional route, a proposed departure window, a pair of airports, and enough tiny notes to make the paper look like it had developed a rash."},{"p":"I took out my pencil."},{"p":"“You may begin,” Pike said."},{"p":"I had imagined this moment approximately ninety times. In some versions I was brilliant. In one particularly ridiculous version Pike shook my hand while an entire room applauded, which was a clue that my imagination needed less free time."},{"p":"In the actual room, I read the first page twice."},{"p":"The departure airport looked straightforward. The destination looked straightforward. The proposed line between them was suspiciously straight. That wasn’t a reason to reject it, but it was a good reason to check what it crossed."},{"p":"I opened the supplied chart extract and traced the route without marking anything yet. The chart showed controlled airspace, a training area, and terrain that rose toward the far side of the route. The packet also included a notice affecting the destination’s runway availability during part of the proposed arrival window."},{"p":"There it was: the kind of detail I’d missed the first time Pike evaluated me."},{"p":"I wanted to circle it immediately and look up at him. Instead I checked the notice’s effective time, compared it with the estimated arrival time, and wrote a question in the margin: **Is the arrival estimate supported by current wind information?**"},{"p":"Pike moved to the window. He didn’t look at my page."},{"p":"The weather information had different issue times. One forecast was newer than another. I marked which report applied to which airport and which period, then checked whether any of the assumptions had been supplied only for the purpose of the exercise."},{"p":"I listed what I knew, then what I still needed to find out."},{"p":"The third page offered a fuel-planning table. It included a sample cruise figure, but not enough information to treat it as an aircraft-specific flight plan. I could compare the fictional options using those assumptions, but I couldn’t use them as performance figures for a real airplane."},{"p":"“Captain Pike?”"},{"p":"He turned."},{"p":"“For this exercise, should I use the supplied performance assumptions only to compare the alternatives?”"},{"p":"“What does the packet say?”"},{"p":"“That they’re simplified training values. Not aircraft performance data.”"},{"p":"“Then you’ve answered your question.”"},{"p":"I almost smiled. Almost."},{"p":"Twenty minutes later, he sat across from me and asked for my recommendation."},{"p":"“I would not approve this route as presented,” I said. “The destination runway restriction could overlap our estimated arrival, and that estimate depends on information I can’t fully verify from the packet. The alternate route avoids one issue but introduces others that need checking. I would hold the decision and get current information before recommending departure.”"},{"p":"“What if the restriction ends ten minutes before your estimated arrival?”"},{"p":"“I’d still want to verify the current status and make sure the timing gives us a reasonable margin. Ten minutes isn’t much if the estimate changes.”"},{"p":"“What if your teammate insists the original route is fine?”"},{"p":"“I’d ask them to show me what supports that conclusion. Then I’d explain what I found.”"},{"p":"“And if they show you something you missed?”"},{"p":"I looked at the sheet. The old version of me might have taken that question as an accusation."},{"p":"“Then we use the better information,” I said."},{"p":"Pike leaned back. “You sound certain.”"},{"p":"My stomach tightened."},{"p":"“About listening to the better information? Yes. About which route we’d actually fly? No. Not yet.”"},{"p":"He gave the smallest nod I had ever seen."},{"p":"Then he reached for my paper."},{"p":"I watched his red pencil move down the margin. He stopped at the runway notice, the weather times, the fuel assumptions. At each place, he made a small mark. He reached the bottom and turned the page over."},{"p":"“There’s a final part,” he said."},{"p":"Of course there was."},{"p":"He put three slips of paper on the table. Each carried a short recommendation supposedly written by a different teammate. One proposed departing as planned. Another proposed a later departure. The third proposed a different destination."},{"p":"“You’ve got three minutes,” Pike said. “Choose one recommendation to present as fairly as you can, even if you disagree with it.”"},{"p":"I chose the later departure. It wasn’t my favorite, but it had a reasonable idea behind it: allow the runway restriction to end before arrival. I explained why somebody might support it, including the chance to gather newer information, then described what would still need checking. I didn’t call the teammate careless. I didn’t call the plan stupid. I didn’t make it sound better than the facts allowed, either."},{"p":"When I finished, Pike asked, “Why that one?”"},{"p":"“Because it solves one problem without pretending it solves all of them.”"},{"p":"He wrote something on his evaluation form."},{"p":"His expression gave nothing away."},{"p":"“Lincoln,” he said, “last time you came in with a method designed to prove that you had completed every step. Today you used a method that helped you notice what you didn’t know. That’s the distinction I was looking for.”"},{"p":"My hands went still."},{"p":"He turned the form so I could read it."},{"p":"**ROUTE VERIFICATION REASSESSMENT: SATISFACTORY.**"},{"p":"I read it twice. For months, I had expected to see the word *conditional* somewhere on that page."},{"p":"“You passed,” Pike said."},{"p":"“I see that.”"},{"p":"“You don’t look particularly pleased.”"},{"p":"“I’m trying not to do anything embarrassing.”"},{"p":"“A sensible objective.”"},{"p":"“It might be too late.”"},{"p":"The corner of his mouth moved."},{"p":"He handed me the form. “This clears the fellowship’s route-verification requirement. It does not certify you to conduct an actual flight, and it has nothing to do with authorizing you to fly solo. Those are separate matters, with separate requirements and your flight instructor’s judgment.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Tell me anyway.”"},{"p":"I did. Mara was responsible for my flight training. Before solo, I would need the applicable knowledge preparation, demonstrated proficiency, and required endorsements. Turning sixteen had only made me old enough to be considered."},{"p":"“Good,” Pike said. “And one more thing.”"},{"p":"I braced myself."},{"p":"“Don’t frame this as the day you stopped making mistakes.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to.”"},{"p":"He glanced at me."},{"p":"“I was going to try not to,” I admitted."},{"p":"“Better.”"},{"p":"Outside, Celia, Nora, and Dev were waiting near the water fountain. Celia was holding a packet of pretzels. Apparently the vending machine had finally surrendered."},{"p":"“Well?” she asked."},{"p":"I held up the form."},{"p":"Nora grinned. Dev gave me a quiet thumbs-up. Celia took one pretzel from the bag and offered it to me."},{"p":"“A ceremonial pretzel,” she said."},{"p":"“Is this a fellowship tradition?”"},{"p":"“It is now.”"},{"p":"I took it. It tasted mostly like salt."},{"p":"That evening I found Mara in the hangar, checking the next day’s schedule. She read the form and smiled."},{"p":"“Well earned,” she said."},{"p":"I waited for the next sentence, the one that would turn my fellowship success into a promise about solo. It didn’t come."},{"p":"Instead Mara asked, “What did you do differently?”"},{"p":"I told her about the blank page, the missing answers, and the three teammates whose recommendations I had to explain fairly."},{"p":"“Sounds useful,” she said."},{"p":"“It was.”"},{"p":"She handed the paper back. “And tomorrow we work on landings.”"},{"p":"“You know how to ruin a victory parade.”"},{"p":"“I prefer to think of it as keeping the parade out of the traffic pattern.”"},{"p":"I laughed, and this time I didn’t mind that she hadn’t said anything about a solo date."},{"p":"The page in my hand had no checkmarks. It had a word I’d worked for, and a list of things I had actually checked."},{"p":"For once, that felt like enough."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twelve — The Airplanes That Waited","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing Dad said when we crossed the Wisconsin state line was, “Nobody is allowed to ask how much longer.”"},{"p":"“How much farther?” I asked."},{"p":"“Different question,” he said. “Same punishment.”"},{"p":"Mom looked up from the passenger seat. “What’s the punishment?”"},{"p":"“I tell them about the history of interstate highway numbering.”"},{"p":"“That isn’t a punishment for Lincoln,” she said. “He’d take notes.”"},{"p":"I considered it, then decided to preserve the peace."},{"p":"The trip to Oshkosh had begun as a birthday surprise. My parents had set aside money for months, booked a modest place to stay, and somehow kept the entire plan secret even though Dad normally couldn’t hide a bag of chips behind his back."},{"p":"We weren’t there for a big airshow. We were there for the EAA Aviation Museum, and for a weekend together before school and training schedules filled the calendar again."},{"p":"The morning we arrived, a breeze pushed loose clouds across the Wisconsin sky. I carried my notebook into the museum and promised myself I wouldn’t write down every airplane I saw."},{"p":"That promise lasted about seven minutes."},{"p":"“You look like you’re inspecting the place,” Mom said as I stopped beside a display about early experimental aircraft."},{"p":"“I’m appreciating the engineering.”"},{"p":"“With your eyebrows?”"},{"p":"I touched the little scar beside my left eyebrow. “These are my engineering eyebrows.”"},{"p":"Dad leaned toward an exhibit label. “We should’ve brought a clipboard.”"},{"p":"Inside, the museum held aircraft from different eras and the stories of people who had built, flown, maintained, and sometimes failed with them. Some were sleek enough to look fast while standing still. Others looked as though a determined person had started with a bicycle and refused to stop adding wings."},{"p":"At one display, Dad stood for a long time reading about the people behind an airplane’s design."},{"p":"“You okay?” I asked."},{"p":"“Yeah. I was just thinking how many tries it must have taken to get some of these ideas right.”"},{"p":"“Probably more than the signs have room for.”"},{"p":"“Exactly. A museum makes history look tidy. The people living it probably had lunch go wrong and lost their keys like everyone else.”"},{"p":"I imagined an early aircraft designer searching through a workshop for a missing wrench while a world-changing airplane waited outside."},{"p":"“Maybe that’s why they invented checklists,” I said."},{"p":"Dad laughed. “Maybe that’s why they invented pockets.”"},{"p":"We moved into another gallery, where a small aircraft stood beneath bright lights. A boy younger than me was studying its landing gear with the concentration of someone trying to solve a mystery."},{"p":"“Do you think that was hard to land?” he asked nobody in particular."},{"p":"I started to answer, then caught myself. I didn’t know that airplane’s handling characteristics, and the exhibit wasn’t a flight manual."},{"p":"“I don’t know,” I said. “But I bet the people who flew it had to learn what it did differently from other airplanes.”"},{"p":"He considered that. “It looks bouncy.”"},{"p":"“It definitely has the appearance of a machine that might object to a bad landing.”"},{"p":"His mother smiled. “He’s building one out of cardboard at home.”"},{"p":"“Tell him to make the landing gear strong,” Dad said. “Our house has survived several cardboard crashes.”"},{"p":"The boy nodded solemnly, as if Dad had delivered classified information."},{"p":"Later we found a quiet place to sit and eat. Mom passed me a sandwich wrapped in paper, and I spread the museum map beside it."},{"p":"“You have the look,” she said."},{"p":"“What look?”"},{"p":"“The one where you’re planning your next six years instead of eating lunch.”"},{"p":"I took a bite to prove her wrong. Mom remained unconvinced."},{"p":"“Pike signed off my reassessment,” I said."},{"p":"“We know,” Dad replied. “You told us in the kitchen, in the car, and again while I was putting gas in the car.”"},{"p":"“I was making sure you understood the importance of the event.”"},{"p":"“The cashier understands too,” Mom said. “She congratulated you on your route verification.”"},{"p":"I groaned. Dad looked delighted."},{"p":"“I keep thinking about what comes next,” I admitted."},{"p":"Mom folded her napkin. “The next thing is probably finishing your sandwich.”"},{"p":"“After that.”"},{"p":"She looked at me for a moment. “What are you hoping comes next?”"},{"p":"“I want to solo. I know it isn’t a prize you get for passing a different test. I know Mara has to see the right things consistently. But I keep imagining it. The empty seat. The airplane feeling different without someone beside me.”"},{"p":"Dad didn’t interrupt."},{"p":"“And then I get worried that I’m imagining it too much,” I added. “Like I’m supposed to stop wanting it until I’m ready.”"},{"p":"“You don’t have to stop wanting something to be patient about it,” Mom said."},{"p":"I looked down at the map. A blue line showed where we’d walked. There were still rooms we hadn’t visited."},{"p":"“How did you two know when you were ready to be parents?” I asked."},{"p":"Dad choked on his water."},{"p":"Mom handed him a napkin. “We didn’t.”"},{"p":"“That’s reassuring.”"},{"p":"“We learned a lot as we went,” she said. “But some decisions require preparation before you take them. You don’t skip that because you’re excited.”"},{"p":"“Or because you’ve already bought the stroller,” Dad added when he could breathe again."},{"p":"We laughed, and the tight feeling in my chest loosened a little."},{"p":"In the afternoon, we spent time in a gallery devoted to homebuilt and experimental aviation. I liked the polished aircraft, but I kept noticing the small details: a shaped fairing, a carefully routed control cable in a display, a photograph of a workshop full of ordinary tools. Those airplanes hadn’t begun as finished machines. Somebody had started with a question and kept working."},{"p":"Mom stopped beside a photograph of a family standing near an aircraft."},{"p":"“That one,” she said. “I like that one.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“Because somebody took the picture. Somebody packed lunch. Somebody probably worried about the weather. You can love flying and still have a life happening around it.”"},{"p":"I looked at the people in the photograph. I’d been thinking about the pilot. I hadn’t thought much about whoever stood behind the camera."},{"p":"At the gift shop, Dad bought a postcard featuring an airplane from the museum. I picked out a small notebook for the next phase of training, though I already owned enough notebooks to supply a very small school."},{"p":"“Another one?” Mom asked."},{"p":"“This one is for important things.”"},{"p":"“And the other seven?”"},{"p":"“Historical records.”"},{"p":"On the drive back to our hotel, my phone buzzed. Imani had sent a picture of Nana June’s tomato plant, now tall enough to need another stake. Below it she wrote: **Grandma says the plant doesn’t care about your schedule.**"},{"p":"I showed Mom, who smiled."},{"p":"I typed back: **Tell her I’m taking notes from the tomato.**"},{"p":"A second message arrived from Rafe. He’d completed the first practice run of his communications demonstration. Beckett had apparently timed the presentation and reported that Rafe used the word *frequency* seventeen times."},{"p":"**Only seventeen?** I wrote. **You’re losing your touch.**"},{"p":"A photo came next: Beckett holding a stopwatch, Rafe pointing at it like an offended game-show contestant. I laughed hard enough that Dad glanced at me in the rearview mirror."},{"p":"“What?”"},{"p":"“My friends are idiots.”"},{"p":"“Good idiots?”"},{"p":"“The best kind.”"},{"p":"That night, after my parents turned out their lights, I opened the new notebook. I expected to write about solo flight. Instead I wrote down three things I’d seen at the museum: a detail somebody had solved, a mistake somebody had learned from, and a person in a photograph who wasn’t the pilot."},{"p":"Then I wrote a fourth thing."},{"p":"**The people who built these airplanes couldn’t skip the parts nobody would see.**"},{"p":"I stared at the sentence. It sounded a little too much like something Uncle Everett would say, which meant I would probably have to endure his approval when I showed him."},{"p":"I closed the notebook and put it on the nightstand."},{"p":"The next morning we’d drive home. Soon after that I’d be back at the airport with Mara, working on the same landings, the same corrections, and the same decisions that sometimes felt small compared with the dream of flying alone."},{"p":"At the museum, I’d seen airplanes that had taken years to become real."},{"p":"I figured I could give mine another few lessons."}]},{"t":"Chapter Thirteen — The Lesson That Clicked","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing I learned after our trip to Wisconsin was that a museum gift-shop notebook did not make you a better pilot."},{"p":"The second thing was that leaving it on your kitchen table made you feel significantly less organized."},{"p":"I discovered both facts on Tuesday morning while Mom drove me to Hampton Roads Executive Airport. My flight bag was between my feet, my headset was in its case, and my brand-new notebook was sitting at home beside a cereal bowl that I had also forgotten to rinse."},{"p":"“You’re staring at your bag,” Mom said."},{"p":"“I’m checking it with my mind.”"},{"p":"“Does that work?”"},{"p":"“So far, it has identified one missing notebook and absolutely no way to retrieve it.”"},{"p":"She laughed. “Use the old one.”"},{"p":"“The old one is full.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“It’s almost full.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“It has room for eight lines, depending on handwriting size.”"},{"p":"“There we go.”"},{"p":"Mara was waiting near the flight school’s office with a weather briefing on her tablet. She asked about Oshkosh before she asked about my notebook. I told her about the homebuilt aircraft and the family photograph, and she listened without looking at her watch."},{"p":"“What stuck with you?” she asked."},{"p":"“How much work happened before anybody saw an airplane fly.”"},{"p":"“Useful thing to notice. Ready to do some of that work?”"},{"p":"I nodded."},{"p":"The weather was suitable for our planned lesson, but suitable didn’t mean identical to the previous week. We reviewed the current conditions and forecast, checked relevant airport information, and discussed what would make us change or cancel the plan. Mara had me talk through our objectives before we went outside."},{"p":"“Traffic-pattern work,” I said. “Stable approaches, consistent airspeed control, landing judgment, and go-arounds when appropriate.”"},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"I knew that look."},{"p":"“Not treating a landing like something I have to save.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"We completed the preflight inspection together, with me doing the work and Mara watching closely enough to catch anything I missed. I checked the items on the aircraft’s checklist rather than relying on a memory I was too proud of. The Cessna 172S looked as it always did: slightly dusty and completely uninterested in my ambitions."},{"p":"After departure, Mara let the quiet settle between us. I had once filled every spare second with questions. Now I was learning that silence could be useful. The airplane had things to tell me if I paid attention."},{"p":"My first approach began well enough. I carried a little extra speed, noticed the runway seeming to slide away beneath us, and felt the old temptation to force the airplane into the shape of a landing."},{"p":"“What are you seeing?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“I’m not where I want to be.”"},{"p":"“Your decision?”"},{"p":"“Go around.”"},{"p":"We did. Mara supervised while I followed the aircraft’s published procedure and her instruction, then rejoined the pattern. My cheeks were warm."},{"p":"“You made that call early,” she said."},{"p":"“It still feels like I wasted an approach.”"},{"p":"“You used an approach to make a good decision. That’s not a waste.”"},{"p":"I wanted to believe her. I also wanted a landing worthy of a framed photograph."},{"p":"It wasn’t."},{"p":"The second approach was stable. I made small corrections rather than waiting for one enormous correction to become necessary. But in the flare, I looked too close to the airplane. We settled more firmly than I’d intended, though within safe limits."},{"p":"“What changed?” Mara asked as we taxied clear."},{"p":"“My eyes. I started staring right in front of the nose.”"},{"p":"“What will you do differently?”"},{"p":"I answered. She didn’t add a speech. We went around again."},{"p":"On the third circuit, something changed—not the wind, not the runway, and definitely not the airplane. I stopped trying to manufacture a perfect landing. I noticed the picture outside, checked the instruments without chasing them, and made smaller adjustments while there was still time for them to matter."},{"p":"Mara said almost nothing."},{"p":"I could feel the airplane responding. Not like it was reading my mind, which would have been terrifying, but like the information I’d spent months collecting had finally begun arriving in the correct order."},{"p":"The runway grew closer. I kept looking where I needed to look. The airplane settled onto the pavement with a gentle, unmistakable touchdown."},{"p":"For a moment I thought we’d bounced, because I was so surprised that the wheels had touched."},{"p":"We hadn’t."},{"p":"“Nice,” Mara said."},{"p":"One word. I grinned as if she’d handed me a trophy."},{"p":"I grinned so hard my cheeks hurt."},{"p":"“Don’t celebrate until we’re clear of the runway,” she added."},{"p":"“I am celebrating internally.”"},{"p":"“Your internal celebration is showing.”"},{"p":"After the appropriate checks and taxi, we set up for another circuit. I expected the magic to disappear. Instead, I made a good approach, handled a small change in the breeze without getting behind the airplane, and landed smoothly again."},{"p":"Not identically. Not perfectly. But calmly."},{"p":"The next one was good too."},{"p":"By the time we shut down, I had stopped counting touchdowns and started noticing something stranger: I hadn’t been afraid of making a correction. I had made corrections early, while they were still small."},{"p":"Inside, Mara pulled up our lesson notes."},{"p":"“You made a real step forward today,” she said."},{"p":"I sat straighter. “I felt it.”"},{"p":"“Tell me what you think happened.”"},{"p":"“I finally figured out how to land?”"},{"p":"She raised an eyebrow."},{"p":"“I know,” I said. “Too dramatic.”"},{"p":"“You connected the sight picture, the airplane’s response, and your timing more consistently. You also made an appropriate go-around decision. That’s meaningful progress.”"},{"p":"“So what’s next?”"},{"p":"“More training.”"},{"p":"“I was hoping for a longer answer.”"},{"p":"“Then: more training, with different conditions, different distractions, and the expectation that you can make sound decisions repeatedly. We’ll continue reviewing the knowledge and procedures required before solo, too.”"},{"p":"I watched her enter notes into my training record."},{"p":"“Was that the best lesson I’ve had?” I asked."},{"p":"“Possibly. But I don’t want you to turn one good lesson into a promise about the next one.”"},{"p":"I looked down at my hands. They were still trembling a little, though not from fear. I was excited enough to run laps around the building."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said. “But can I be happy about it?”"},{"p":"Mara smiled. “Please do.”"},{"p":"I called Uncle Everett from the parking lot while Mom finished a work call in the car."},{"p":"“Three good landings,” I told him before he could say hello."},{"p":"“Hello to you too.”"},{"p":"“And I made a go-around on the first approach.”"},{"p":"“Sounds like a productive lesson.”"},{"p":"“It felt different.”"},{"p":"“Good different?”"},{"p":"“Like I was finally ahead of the airplane instead of apologizing to it afterward.”"},{"p":"He laughed. “That’s a description I haven’t heard before.”"},{"p":"“You can use it.”"},{"p":"“I’ll cite my source.”"},{"p":"I wanted him to tell me I was close. I almost asked. But the question stayed in my throat, partly because I knew his answer. He wasn’t my instructor, and he couldn’t decide when I was ready."},{"p":"Instead, I told him about Mara’s reminder not to turn one lesson into a promise."},{"p":"“She’s right,” he said. “And you can still be proud. Those things aren’t opposites.”"},{"p":"When Mom finished her call, she asked how it went. I told her everything, including the first approach that ended in a go-around."},{"p":"“You sound happy,” she said."},{"p":"“I am.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"“Also, I left the new notebook at home.”"},{"p":"“I noticed.”"},{"p":"“You could have told me.”"},{"p":"“You were busy checking your bag with your mind.”"},{"p":"That evening I opened the notebook at last. On the first page, I wrote: **Tuesday: the lesson that clicked.**"},{"p":"Underneath it, after thinking for a while, I added: **Now find out if it still clicks on Thursday.**"},{"p":"For once, that second sentence didn’t spoil the first."}]},{"t":"Chapter Fourteen — The Empty Chair","blocks":[{"p":"By Thursday morning, I had replayed Tuesday’s landings about six hundred times."},{"p":"This was not a scientifically verified number. It might have been closer to nine hundred."},{"p":"At breakfast, Dad watched me use a piece of toast to demonstrate an imaginary approach over the butter dish."},{"p":"“I assume the toast has been cleared to land?” he asked."},{"p":"“It’s on final.”"},{"p":"“The butter dish has a runway closure.”"},{"p":"I diverted to the jam."},{"p":"Mom set down her coffee. “Please don’t declare an emergency over the preserves.”"},{"p":"I laughed, but after breakfast I stood in the hallway with my flight bag and checked its pockets three times. The notebook was there. So was my logbook. My headset. My pencil. Everything that belonged in the bag."},{"p":"The thing I couldn’t check was whether Tuesday had been real."},{"p":"At the airport, Mara noticed before I said anything."},{"p":"“You look like you’re about to take an exam,” she said."},{"p":"“I was hoping for another good lesson.”"},{"p":"“That’s reasonable.”"},{"p":"“I was hoping for exactly the same lesson.”"},{"p":"“That’s less reasonable.”"},{"p":"She led me to a table beside the office window. Outside, a maintenance worker wheeled a small cart past the hangar. Someone inside was arguing gently with a printer."},{"p":"“Tell me what you’re worried about,” she said."},{"p":"“That Tuesday was luck. That I’ll get in the airplane and forget how to do everything.”"},{"p":"“Have you forgotten everything before?”"},{"p":"“Not everything. Just the parts I needed at the time.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"I looked up."},{"p":"“We aren’t trying to reproduce Tuesday. We’re trying to see how you fly today.”"},{"p":"That sounded sensible. Unfortunately, sensible was not the same as comforting."},{"p":"We reviewed the weather and the lesson plan. Conditions were acceptable for dual instruction, but the wind was different enough that I couldn’t simply replay the picture from Tuesday. Mara reminded me that our decisions would follow the actual conditions, not the schedule or my hopes."},{"p":"In the airplane, my first landing was merely okay. I corrected too late for a slight drift and touched down less smoothly than I wanted. Mara had been ready to assist, but I maintained control and followed her directions."},{"p":"My stomach sank."},{"p":"“Well,” I said as we taxied, “there goes the miracle.”"},{"p":"“What miracle?”"},{"p":"“Tuesday.”"},{"p":"“Tuesday happened. Thursday is happening now.”"},{"p":"The second approach was better until I caught myself thinking about how good it was. Then I became stiff, held the controls too tightly, and began making corrections that were larger than necessary."},{"p":"“What are you doing?” Mara asked, not sharply."},{"p":"“Trying to keep it perfect.”"},{"p":"“Is that helping?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"I recognized the approach was no longer meeting the standard we’d discussed and elected to go around. For once, I didn’t argue with myself about it."},{"p":"Back in the pattern, I tried something I’d learned from Pike’s fellowship exercises. I said what I knew and what I didn’t."},{"p":"“I know I can make the corrections,” I said. “I don’t know why I’m rushing them.”"},{"p":"“What do you think you’re protecting?” Mara asked."},{"p":"I knew the answer immediately. I didn’t want to say it."},{"p":"“Tuesday,” I admitted."},{"p":"She nodded. “You can’t damage Tuesday by having a different Thursday.”"},{"p":"We continued, and the next landing was steady. Not as pretty as the best one from Tuesday. More important, I wasn’t trying to impress anybody. I watched, adjusted, and accepted the result."},{"p":"After shutdown, Mara asked me to join her in the briefing room. She placed an empty chair on the opposite side of the table."},{"p":"“That’s dramatic,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s a chair.”"},{"p":"“It’s an empty chair positioned very deliberately.”"},{"p":"“Fine. It’s a dramatic chair.”"},{"p":"She sat across from me. “When you’re flying with me, you know I can help. Even if I say nothing, you know I’m here. What changes when I’m not?”"},{"p":"“The airplane’s lighter,” I said."},{"p":"“True. And?”"},{"p":"“There’s no one to catch a mistake.”"},{"p":"“That’s one reason we don’t authorize a solo until the student demonstrates the necessary proficiency, judgment, and required knowledge. We also have to make sure all the applicable certificates, medical qualification, training, and endorsements are in place. None of those is replaced by a birthday or a good landing.”"},{"p":"I nodded. We’d discussed those requirements before, but the empty chair made them feel less like a checklist on paper."},{"p":"“I don’t want you imagining that a first solo means being fearless,” she said. “I want you to be able to notice nerves without letting them make your decisions.”"},{"p":"“How?”"},{"p":"“Start by telling the truth about them. What were you thinking on that second approach?”"},{"p":"“That I had to prove Tuesday wasn’t luck.”"},{"p":"“And what did that thought make you do?”"},{"p":"“Rush. Overcorrect. Try to rescue a landing that didn’t need rescuing until I started interfering.”"},{"p":"“You recognized it and went around. That’s good judgment. But we still have work to do.”"},{"p":"She didn’t offer a solo date. I didn’t ask for one."},{"p":"On the drive home, I called Rafe. He answered on the fourth ring with a loud burst of static made entirely by his mouth."},{"p":"“This is Flight Service Radio Guy. State your request.”"},{"p":"“I request a different friend.”"},{"p":"“Denied. All other friends are unavailable.”"},{"p":"I told him about the lesson, including the dramatic chair."},{"p":"“That’s actually a little scary,” he said."},{"p":"“The chair?”"},{"p":"“The idea of being the only one up there.”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"He was quiet for a moment. Rafe was rarely quiet. I considered checking whether the call had dropped."},{"p":"“When I did the communications demonstration,” he said, “I practiced the script so many times I could say it in my sleep. Then I stood in front of people and forgot the first sentence.”"},{"p":"“What’d you do?”"},{"p":"“Looked at my notes. Started again. Nobody burst into flames.”"},{"p":"“Excellent safety record.”"},{"p":"“Thank you. The point is, I kept thinking a mistake meant I didn’t belong there. But I did belong there. I just needed the notes.”"},{"p":"“An airplane is a little different from a presentation.”"},{"p":"“Obviously. Your audience can fall out of the sky.”"},{"p":"“Rafe.”"},{"p":"“Sorry. Bad joke. I’m saying nerves don’t tell you whether you belong. They’re just nerves.”"},{"p":"After we hung up, I sat on my bed with my logbook open. I had written down what went well and what needed work. I started to draw a little star beside Tuesday’s entry, then stopped."},{"p":"Not because it hadn’t been a great lesson. It had."},{"p":"But I didn’t want to spend the rest of my training trying to get back to one Tuesday morning."},{"p":"Downstairs, Dad was putting away groceries. Mom was sorting mail at the counter. I could hear them talking about whether we needed more dish soap, a question with no relationship to aviation and apparently no easy answer."},{"p":"I went down to help."},{"p":"“How was flying?” Dad asked."},{"p":"“Not as smooth as Tuesday.”"},{"p":"He waited."},{"p":"“But I learned something.”"},{"p":"“Want to tell me?”"},{"p":"I took the bag with the dish soap and put it under the sink."},{"p":"“I think I was trying to make every landing prove I was ready.”"},{"p":"“And now?”"},{"p":"“Now I think I need to keep doing the work until the flying proves it without me arguing.”"},{"p":"Dad considered that. “Sounds like a better system.”"},{"p":"He handed me a bag of apples. I put them in the bowl, one at a time."},{"p":"Later, alone in my room, I looked at a photograph on my phone from the museum. Mom had taken it without telling me. I was standing beside a display, reading every word on the sign. Dad was just visible in the corner, holding our jackets. Neither of them was looking at the camera."},{"p":"I thought about Mara’s empty chair, Pike’s marker, Uncle Everett’s patient phone calls, and my friends’ ridiculous messages. I thought about all the people who had helped me reach a point where flying alone was even a possibility."},{"p":"I still wanted that flight more than anything."},{"p":"When I looked at that empty chair again in my mind, I could still picture everyone who had helped me get this far."}]},{"t":"Chapter Fifteen — The Things I Could Control","blocks":[{"p":"The following Tuesday morning, I made a list titled THINGS I CAN CONTROL."},{"p":"The title took up nearly half the top line because I wrote it in capital letters. This was probably unnecessary. Nobody else was going to read the list unless Rafe broke into my room, and if Rafe broke into my room, I had bigger problems than stationery."},{"p":"I wrote:"},{"p":"Get enough sleep."},{"p":"Eat breakfast."},{"p":"Check the weather before leaving."},{"p":"Show up prepared."},{"p":"Tell Mara when something feels wrong."},{"p":"Then I sat with the pencil hovering above the paper."},{"p":"Make every landing perfect seemed like an obvious addition, except it was also the exact sort of thinking that had turned my last lesson into an argument with myself at a thousand feet."},{"p":"I crossed out the word perfect before I’d even written it."},{"p":"At breakfast, Dad read the list upside down from across the table."},{"p":"“Is ‘eat breakfast’ on there?” he asked."},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“Good. You’re already achieving your goals.”"},{"p":"Mom leaned over my shoulder. “You could add ‘leave early enough that traffic doesn’t become an emergency.’”"},{"p":"“It’s a twenty-minute drive.”"},{"p":"“Which is why leaving twenty minutes before you’re supposed to be there isn’t a plan.”"},{"p":"She was right. Again. Nobody in our house bothered keeping statistics anymore."},{"p":"I added Leave with extra time."},{"p":"At the airport, Mara was standing beside the Cessna with a clipboard and an expression I had learned not to interpret. She could wear that same expression when she was about to praise me, challenge me, or tell me I’d left my water bottle on the wing."},{"p":"“How are you feeling?” she asked."},{"p":"“Prepared. A little nervous.”"},{"p":"“Good answer. What did you do with the nerves?”"},{"p":"I showed her the list."},{"p":"She read it without smiling. That was fine. I hadn’t written it for comedy, although Dad had apparently disagreed."},{"p":"“This is useful,” she said. “What happens if you still feel nervous after doing everything on it?”"},{"p":"“I fly anyway?”"},{"p":"“Maybe. Maybe not. You assess whether you’re fit to fly, and we make that decision together today. A checklist doesn’t force a yes.”"},{"p":"I looked at the paper again. I had made a list of things I could do. I hadn’t made a rule that said doing them guaranteed anything."},{"p":"“I think I wanted a way to make the nerves go away,” I admitted."},{"p":"“A routine can help you notice what’s happening and prepare properly. It isn’t a switch that turns feelings off.”"},{"p":"Before the flight, we reviewed the weather and airport information, the airplane’s status, and what we intended to practice. Mara explained that she’d introduce a few ordinary training changes along the way. Nothing mysterious, she promised. She wasn’t going to spring a movie-style engine failure on me while wearing a villain’s mustache."},{"p":"“That seems like a very specific promise,” I said."},{"p":"“I’ve seen your imagination at work.”"},{"p":"The first part of the lesson felt almost disappointingly normal. We completed the preflight inspection together, used the appropriate checklists, and departed for the practice area. Mara had me hold an assigned altitude and heading while she asked questions about our position and the conditions around us."},{"p":"I answered one question too quickly."},{"p":"“Where would you look to verify that?” she asked."},{"p":"I told her, then checked."},{"p":"My first answer had been correct, but it bothered me that I had guessed before verifying. I thought of Pike and his habit of leaving a marker uncapped until somebody proved they deserved to write something down."},{"p":"“You didn’t need to catch me there,” I said."},{"p":"“No. But you needed to catch yourself.”"},{"p":"Later, Mara changed our plan. Instead of continuing the original exercise, we would return to the airport and work in the traffic pattern. She asked me to explain the change in my own words, including what we needed to consider before rejoining airport traffic."},{"p":"I could feel my brain trying to race ahead to the landing."},{"p":"One thing at a time, I told myself."},{"p":"I took a moment to organize the situation. We knew where we were. We had the current airport information. We had time to communicate and plan without rushing. I explained the broad sequence and checked it with Mara."},{"p":"“All right,” she said. “Let’s do that.”"},{"p":"Our first approach was steady until I noticed we were not where I wanted to be. My old instinct arrived immediately: fix it, fix it, fix it, before Mara thinks you can’t."},{"p":"I recognized the thought because it had practically rented a room in my head."},{"p":"Instead of chasing the airplane around with bigger and bigger corrections, I told Mara what I was seeing and made a measured adjustment. The approach settled down. We landed safely, though not as smoothly as those Tuesday landings I kept replaying."},{"p":"“How was that?” Mara asked as we taxied clear."},{"p":"“Safe. Not especially pretty.”"},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"“I noticed what was changing before it became a surprise.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “That’s worth paying attention to.”"},{"p":"The next circuit gave me a different problem. I was so pleased with recognizing the first one that I started planning the next landing before we had even finished climbing away. My attention drifted. Mara asked a simple question about our position, and I took too long to answer."},{"p":"“I’m ahead of myself,” I said."},{"p":"“Then come back to where we are.”"},{"p":"Not dramatic advice, but it worked."},{"p":"We completed another approach. On the following one, I judged that our approach wasn’t stable enough to continue safely and announced a go-around. Mara confirmed and supervised as I followed the trained procedure."},{"p":"I expected the familiar pinch of disappointment."},{"p":"It came, but smaller this time. I hadn’t lost an argument with the runway. I’d made a decision based on what the airplane was doing."},{"p":"After we shut down, Mara asked me to write three things that had gone well and three things that needed work. I wrote them in the same notebook as my morning list."},{"p":"One of my strengths was recognizing a developing problem early. One weakness was getting distracted by the next task before finishing the current one."},{"p":"Another was how easily I measured a whole lesson by its smoothest landing."},{"p":"“You’re being more specific,” Mara said, reading over my shoulder with permission. “That’s useful.”"},{"p":"“Does it mean I’m getting close?”"},{"p":"She closed the clipboard, but not the conversation."},{"p":"“It means you’re making progress. We’re looking for sound judgment and consistent performance across lessons, not a single good morning. There’s more ground work and supervised flying ahead.”"},{"p":"I resisted searching her answer for hidden meaning. Mara generally said what she meant."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said."},{"p":"“And Lincoln?”"},{"p":"“Yeah?”"},{"p":"“You don’t have to pretend not to care.”"},{"p":"That surprised me."},{"p":"“I care a lot.”"},{"p":"“I know. The trick is caring enough to prepare without caring so much about the outcome that you stop paying attention to the airplane.”"},{"p":"At home, I taped the list inside my closet door, where I would see it every morning but nobody else would have to admire my handwriting."},{"p":"Then I added one more line:"},{"p":"Notice what is happening now."},{"p":"I stood back to read it. My list wasn’t a secret formula for becoming brave. It was a reminder that even when I couldn’t control the wind, the runway, or the nervous little voice in my head, I could choose what I paid attention to next."},{"p":"I left the list where I could see it the next morning."}]},{"t":"Chapter Sixteen — No Gold Stars for Guessing","blocks":[{"p":"The following Tuesday, Beckett handed me a tiny plastic airplane with one wing painted bright orange."},{"p":"“What’s this?” I asked."},{"p":"“An airplane.”"},{"p":"“I was hoping for a more advanced explanation.”"},{"p":"“It’s a reminder not to overcorrect.”"},{"p":"He set it on the picnic table outside the academy and demonstrated by pushing one wingtip down. The model rocked back the other way when he let go."},{"p":"“The first version had a loose connection,” he explained. “I kept adjusting everything else to make it behave. Turned out I needed to fix the connection, not keep adding more fixes.”"},{"p":"“You’re giving me an airplane-shaped lecture.”"},{"p":"“Correct. It’s portable.”"},{"p":"I turned the model in my hand. Beckett had filed the edges smooth, but there was a faint smear of glue under the orange wing. He had probably noticed it a hundred times. He hadn’t tried to hide it."},{"p":"“Thanks,” I said."},{"p":"“If you break it, I charge for repairs.”"},{"p":"“How much?”"},{"p":"“One sandwich.”"},{"p":"“You have an alarming business model.”"},{"p":"He grinned and headed toward the workshop, where an instructor had promised to let him help inventory tools. Beckett had begun talking about maintenance careers with the kind of careful excitement I used to reserve for flying. I liked hearing it. I liked that his plans were taking shape in a different direction from mine."},{"p":"Inside, Mara had laid out a short ground review. She asked about airport operations, aircraft limitations, emergency priorities, and decisions a student pilot might face before a first solo. This conversation was a review, not a substitute for the required pre-solo knowledge test or training endorsements. Mara had explained those requirements before, and today she was interested in how I thought."},{"p":"“What would make you postpone a flight?” she asked."},{"p":"“Weather outside safe limits. An aircraft issue. Not feeling well or being too distracted to fly safely. Missing required documents or authorizations.”"},{"p":"“What if the airplane is fine and the weather is legal, but you’re not comfortable with the conditions?”"},{"p":"“I’d tell you and not go until we’d figured out why.”"},{"p":"“Good. Legal doesn’t always mean wise for the pilot and situation.”"},{"p":"She gave me a scenario involving a change in the wind and a runway that would require a different plan. I talked through what information I would verify and how I would decide whether the operation remained suitable for my experience."},{"p":"I made one assumption without saying it aloud."},{"p":"Mara noticed."},{"p":"“Where did that number come from?”"},{"p":"I looked at the page. “My head.”"},{"p":"“Reliable source?”"},{"p":"“Mixed reviews.”"},{"p":"“Then let’s use the aircraft information and current conditions instead.”"},{"p":"We both smiled. I corrected the assumption and finished the discussion without feeling as if the mistake had ruined the morning."},{"p":"The flight that followed was uneventful, which I was beginning to appreciate."},{"p":"Mara observed while I managed familiar tasks, maintained awareness of the airport environment, and explained my decisions when there was time to talk. We flew several traffic patterns. The landings were not identical, but they were controlled. Between landings, I concentrated on the next approach instead of ranking the last one."},{"p":"Then the wind shifted enough to change the feel of the approach."},{"p":"My hands tightened. I noticed it, relaxed my grip, and focused on the references and aircraft response we’d practiced. I made small corrections instead of trying to win an argument with every bump."},{"p":"We landed, taxied clear, and continued the lesson."},{"p":"I waited for Mara to say something extraordinary."},{"p":"She asked whether I’d remembered to check the next taxi instruction."},{"p":"I had."},{"p":"On our final approach, I was a little high and not satisfied with how the picture was developing. I made the decision to go around before the situation became urgent. Mara supervised without taking over."},{"p":"There it was again: the runway sliding beneath us instead of receiving our wheels. But I didn’t feel as if it had rejected me. We had more flying to do, and the safest choice was to keep flying."},{"p":"After shutdown, Mara and I sat in the quiet cockpit for a moment. The engine ticked softly as it cooled."},{"p":"“You’ve shown me more consistent decision-making over the last few lessons,” she said."},{"p":"My stomach did something complicated."},{"p":"“Does that mean—”"},{"p":"“It means we’re moving into the next phase of preparation.”"},{"p":"I tried not to grin like somebody had just opened a refrigerator full of my favorite food."},{"p":"“What phase?”"},{"p":"“We’ll continue the required pre-solo training and ground work, including the knowledge test and endorsements when appropriate. I need to see you handle the full range of required maneuvers and procedures safely and consistently. We’ll also review conditions and your readiness on the day. Nothing about this is automatic.”"},{"p":"I nodded. Then I nodded again, because apparently one nod had not been enough."},{"p":"“But,” she added, “if you keep demonstrating this level of judgment and improve the areas we’ve identified, a first solo is becoming a realistic next milestone.”"},{"p":"The words were quieter than I had imagined they’d be."},{"p":"No dramatic music. No applause from the hangar. Just Mara speaking plainly beside an airplane that smelled faintly of warm metal and sun-baked upholstery."},{"p":"“I don’t want to rush it,” I said."},{"p":"“I believe you.”"},{"p":"“I also really want to rush it.”"},{"p":"“I believe that too.”"},{"p":"We laughed."},{"p":"Later that afternoon, I met Imani at the library. She had spread weather notes across a table, with little colored tabs sticking out like flags."},{"p":"“You look like a very organized porcupine,” I said."},{"p":"“Thank you. I’ve worked hard on my personal style.”"},{"p":"I told her what Mara had said. I tried to sound casual and failed immediately."},{"p":"“That’s big,” Imani said."},{"p":"“It’s not a date.”"},{"p":"“I heard you.”"},{"p":"“And she said there are more requirements.”"},{"p":"“I heard that too.”"},{"p":"“And—”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"I stopped."},{"p":"“You’re allowed to be happy about making progress,” she said. “You don’t have to turn it into either a guarantee or a warning.”"},{"p":"She pushed one of her weather sheets toward me. It showed observations she’d been collecting, including a few readings that hadn’t matched what she expected."},{"p":"“Look at Thursday,” she said. “I thought my sensor was wrong because the number surprised me. So I checked it against another source and found out the conditions really had changed.”"},{"p":"“You didn’t just erase the reading?”"},{"p":"“I considered it. My notebook was going to look much nicer without it.”"},{"p":"“Tragic.”"},{"p":"“But then it wouldn’t be a notebook about weather. It would be a notebook about what I wanted the weather to do.”"},{"p":"I studied the page. She’d written a careful note beside the unusual measurement, explaining what she’d checked and what remained uncertain."},{"p":"It reminded me of the notes Pike had made me defend, and of the corrections in my flight notebook."},{"p":"“Nana June still helping?” I asked."},{"p":"“She supervised from a lawn chair yesterday. Said my equipment was blocking her tomatoes’ view of the sun.”"},{"p":"“Can tomatoes see?”"},{"p":"“According to Nana June, they see everything.”"},{"p":"We laughed, and I didn’t ask for details about her grandmother’s health. Imani could tell me what she wanted to tell me. That was something I’d learned the hard way, and it mattered more than my curiosity."},{"p":"When I got home, I found Uncle Everett’s number in my phone and sent him a message: Mara says solo is becoming a realistic next milestone. Still lots to do."},{"p":"He replied a few minutes later."},{"p":"Good. Keep doing the lots to do."},{"p":"Then another message arrived."},{"p":"And remember to enjoy it."},{"p":"I placed Beckett’s orange-winged airplane on my desk beside the photograph from Oshkosh. My notebook was open to a page full of ordinary details: weather checked, decisions explained, approaches assessed, mistakes noticed, work remaining."},{"p":"I had once imagined that being told I was getting close would make the nerves disappear."},{"p":"Instead, they were still there, mixed with excitement and a kind of gratitude I couldn’t quite fit into words."},{"p":"For the first time, I didn’t feel an urgent need to get rid of either feeling."},{"p":"I set my alarm and closed the notebook."},{"p":"I had a plan for tomorrow. That was enough for tonight."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seventeen — The Test with My Name on It","blocks":[{"p":"The first time Mara handed me a test with my name at the top, I felt strangely relieved."},{"p":"Paper was familiar territory. Paper couldn’t bounce on landing, drift away from the centerline, or surprise you with a gust from the left. Paper stayed where you put it, unless Rafe borrowed it and folded it into an airplane that probably violated several laws of physics."},{"p":"Then I read the first question and discovered that paper could absolutely make your palms sweat."},{"p":"We were in the small classroom beside the flight school’s office. The whiteboard still held a drawing of a traffic pattern from somebody else’s lesson. One of the arrows had been drawn so thickly it looked like a giant worm trying to escape the runway."},{"p":"“Before we start,” Mara said, “tell me what this is.”"},{"p":"“A test?”"},{"p":"“An excellent start. More specifically?”"},{"p":"“The required pre-solo aeronautical knowledge test.”"},{"p":"“Right. For your solo preparation, it needs to address the applicable regulations, the airspace and procedures where you’ll fly, and the flight characteristics and operational limitations of the airplane you’ll use. You’ll need to show satisfactory knowledge and we’ll review anything you miss.”"},{"p":"I looked at the pages again. “So not a pop quiz about the history of the propeller?”"},{"p":"“I know how disappointed you are.”"},{"p":"“Devastated.”"},{"p":"She set a pencil beside the test. “This is not the FAA private pilot knowledge exam. That’s a separate requirement later in your training. Today is about what you must understand before I can authorize you to fly alone. Take your time. If something is unclear, tell me.”"},{"p":"I wanted to say I wasn’t nervous, but my knee was bouncing under the table like it had accepted a job as a sewing machine."},{"p":"Instead, I put both feet on the floor and opened my notebook to the first page of the routine I’d been using."},{"p":"What do I know? What do I need to check? What am I assuming?"},{"p":"I couldn’t use my notebook to answer the test. That wasn’t the point. But I could use the questions to settle my thinking before I began."},{"p":"I wrote my name in the space provided. It looked like the signature of a person who had just been told his pencil might explode."},{"p":"The early questions were familiar. I recognized the applicable operating limitations we’d reviewed for our Cessna 172S, the need to understand the airport environment, and the responsibilities that came with being the only person aboard. Others required more thought. A question about a changing wind forced me to slow down and separate what I’d been told from what I actually knew. One about a nearby airspace boundary made me picture the chart instead of relying on a half-remembered rule."},{"p":"I got stuck on a scenario involving an unfamiliar instruction while taxiing."},{"p":"My first impulse was to select the answer that sounded most confident."},{"p":"That had been a dangerous little habit of mine. Confidence was useful when it came after understanding. It was less useful when it arrived first and demanded a parking space."},{"p":"I read the question again. The right response was to stop in a safe place if necessary and clarify rather than move an airplane while uncertain about the clearance or instruction."},{"p":"I circled my answer and moved on."},{"p":"When I finished, Mara didn’t snatch up the pages and announce a score like a game-show host. She sat across from me, marked the test, and turned it back so we could look at it together."},{"p":"“You missed two,” she said."},{"p":"My stomach dropped. “Is that—”"},{"p":"“We’re going to review them. That’s what matters next.”"},{"p":"One mistake came from confusing two details about our local procedures. The other was a question about an aircraft limitation I had answered from memory without checking whether I was thinking of the correct configuration."},{"p":"Mara opened the appropriate reference material and had me work through both items. She asked me to explain not only the correct answer but how I would find it if I wasn’t sure."},{"p":"I talked too fast on the first one. She waited until I noticed and started again."},{"p":"On the second, I admitted I’d pictured a different number because it was the one I’d practiced most recently."},{"p":"“That’s why we review,” she said. “An answer you can repeat isn’t always an answer you understand.”"},{"p":"When she was satisfied that I’d corrected the gaps, she documented the test and our review as part of my training records."},{"p":"“Does that mean I passed?” I asked."},{"p":"“You’ve satisfactorily completed this knowledge requirement,” she said. “It doesn’t mean we’re finished preparing you to solo.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"She gave me a look."},{"p":"“I know it with my brain,” I amended. “The rest of me is still catching up.”"},{"p":"“Fair enough.”"},{"p":"Outside, the afternoon sun made the ramp shimmer. Mara and I walked to the airplane, and I noticed that my nerves hadn’t vanished after the test. They’d just changed clothes. Now they were dressed for flying."},{"p":"The lesson was a series of supervised exercises that asked me to do several ordinary things well, not one impressive thing perfectly. We reviewed our plan, checked the current conditions and aircraft status, and discussed what would make us stop or change it. Mara kept the decisions appropriate to the lesson and remained ready to intervene."},{"p":"In the air, she introduced variations without turning the flight into a surprise contest. We practiced normal operations, traffic awareness, and a simulated problem handled under her supervision."},{"p":"She wanted to hear what I noticed and why I chose a response."},{"p":"At one point I reached for an answer too quickly."},{"p":"“What did you leave out?” she asked."},{"p":"I looked outside, then at the instruments, then at the situation again. “I haven’t confirmed what changed.”"},{"p":"“Then start there.”"},{"p":"On the next approach, a small gust moved us away from the picture I wanted. I corrected, waited, and made another measured adjustment instead of chasing the runway back and forth. It wasn’t as smooth as the remarkable lesson two weeks earlier, but it was controlled and understandable. Mara said very little until we were on the ground."},{"p":"“You made the corrections you needed,” she said during the debrief. “You also recognized when the airplane wasn’t exactly where you wanted it. That’s a useful combination.”"},{"p":"“Useful enough?”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“Sorry. My mouth asked before my brain approved the question.”"},{"p":"She smiled. “Useful enough to build on. We still need more than one day’s evidence.”"},{"p":"I knew that, but I still caught myself listening for a date."},{"p":"That evening, Dad found me at the kitchen table studying the notes from the lesson. He was carrying two bowls of popcorn, which was an excellent way to begin almost any conversation."},{"p":"“You look like you’re trying to negotiate with a notebook,” he said."},{"p":"“It isn’t being reasonable.”"},{"p":"He set a bowl beside me. “You finished the knowledge test today, right?”"},{"p":"“Yes. And reviewed the mistakes.”"},{"p":"“That sounds like progress.”"},{"p":"“It is. It’s just not the kind that comes with a date.”"},{"p":"Dad pulled out a chair. “At the library, we have a summer reading challenge. Some kids want to know exactly how many books they have to read to earn the prize.”"},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"“The funny part is that the ones who find books they actually enjoy usually stop counting so much.”"},{"p":"I considered that. “Are you comparing flying to reading?”"},{"p":"“I’m a librarian. It’s my legal obligation.”"},{"p":"I ate a handful of popcorn. “I do enjoy it. Even when I’m nervous.”"},{"p":"“Good. I hope you remember that part.”"},{"p":"After he went upstairs, I looked again at the page where Mara had written the two things I needed to review. The mistakes weren’t crossed out so heavily that they disappeared. They were still there, with corrections beside them."},{"p":"For once, I liked the page better that way."},{"p":"It showed where I’d been and what I’d done about it."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eighteen — What the Other Seat Is For","blocks":[{"p":"Uncle Everett arrived on Saturday morning with a paper bag of pastries and the expression of a man who knew he had bought the good ones."},{"p":"“I’ve brought provisions,” he announced from the front porch."},{"p":"Mom took the bag. “You’ve brought powdered sugar into a house with dark furniture.”"},{"p":"“Every operation has risks.”"},{"p":"“You can manage this one with a plate.”"},{"p":"He saluted her with two fingers and followed me into the kitchen."},{"p":"Everett had flown Navy fighters before I was born, which meant he had enough stories to fill several books and enough judgment not to tell most of them when my parents were within earshot. He wasn’t my flight instructor. He never pretended to be. But he understood something about being responsible for an airplane that I was still trying to put into words."},{"p":"We took our pastries onto the back steps. The air was warm, and somewhere beyond the fence a lawn mower was making a noise like an angry vacuum cleaner."},{"p":"“Mara says I’m getting closer,” I told him."},{"p":"“I heard.”"},{"p":"“From me?”"},{"p":"“You sent three messages. The second had seven exclamation points.”"},{"p":"“I was being restrained.”"},{"p":"“Naturally.”"},{"p":"I explained the knowledge test, the review, and the recent flying. I told him about the lesson where everything had clicked, and about the less wonderful one afterward when my nerves had returned like relatives who didn’t understand the phrase ‘we should get going.’"},{"p":"Everett listened without interrupting. When I finally stopped, he brushed sugar from his thumb."},{"p":"“What are you most worried about?” he asked."},{"p":"I stared at the yard. “That Mara will think I’m ready, and then I’ll get into the airplane and discover I’m not the person she thought I was.”"},{"p":"He didn’t answer right away."},{"p":"“That’s a pretty specific fear,” he said."},{"p":"“I’ve had time to decorate it.”"},{"p":"“Sounds like it.”"},{"p":"I waited for the story about a carrier landing in terrible weather, or the time some famous pilot had been scared and conquered it through sheer determination. Instead, he pointed at the bag."},{"p":"“Hand me another pastry.”"},{"p":"“That’s your advice?”"},{"p":"“I’m building suspense.”"},{"p":"I gave him the last cinnamon twist."},{"p":"“Listen,” he said. “You don’t get ready by becoming a different person. You get ready by doing the work until the person you already are can handle the responsibility. Mara knows your strengths and your rough spots. That’s why she’s teaching you instead of asking for a dramatic speech.”"},{"p":"“But what if I feel nervous on the day?”"},{"p":"“Then you’ll tell her how you’re feeling, assess whether you’re fit to fly, and make a sound decision together. Nerves tell you something. You still have to decide whether you’re fit to fly.”"},{"p":"I rubbed the crescent scar near my left eyebrow, something I did when I was thinking harder than I wanted anyone to notice."},{"p":"“Were you nervous before your first solo?”"},{"p":"“Of course.”"},{"p":"“Really?”"},{"p":"“Lincoln, I was young, not made of aluminum.”"},{"p":"That made me laugh."},{"p":"“And when you started flying bigger, faster airplanes?” I asked."},{"p":"“Different airplane, different training, different responsibilities. Same rule about respecting what you don’t know.”"},{"p":"He leaned back against the step. “There’s another thing. The person in the other seat isn’t there to make you look good. A good instructor is there to teach, correct, and decide when you can safely handle the airplane without them. Don’t spend your energy trying to impress Mara. Spend it showing her what you actually do.”"},{"p":"I thought about the times I’d pushed too hard on an approach because I wanted a pretty landing. The times I’d rushed to answer Pike because silence felt like failure. The times I’d turned a perfectly normal lesson into a private contest that nobody else knew was happening."},{"p":"“That is annoyingly sensible,” I said."},{"p":"“I save my best material for when there are pastries.”"},{"p":"Later, at the airport, Mara asked me to describe my preparation routine before we went flying. I told her how I checked the practical things first: rest, food, how I felt, whether I was distracted. Then I reviewed the lesson objectives and the conditions we’d be dealing with, leaving room for the plan to change."},{"p":"“And if you don’t feel ready on a particular day?” she asked."},{"p":"“I say so.”"},{"p":"“Even if you’ve been looking forward to it?”"},{"p":"“Especially then.”"},{"p":"She nodded. “Good. Today we’re still flying together.”"},{"p":"We were. I made myself hear the sentence without adding the word unfortunately."},{"p":"The flight was busy in the ordinary way that required steady attention. We worked through procedures Mara had already taught me, and she evaluated how I handled changes without giving me a script for every moment. I kept my eyes moving, checked my assumptions, and said aloud when I needed a moment to think. Once, I caught myself trying to hurry because I knew she was watching. I slowed down instead."},{"p":"On one approach, I didn’t like how things were developing and went around. On another, I held a more stable picture and completed a landing that felt almost boring."},{"p":"Almost boring was wonderful."},{"p":"We flew another circuit. Mara said less than she had during earlier lessons. Her quiet used to make me panic. Now I understood that she was observing, not setting a trap."},{"p":"Back on the ramp, we secured the airplane and went inside to debrief. Mara spread out her notes and asked me to evaluate the flight before she shared her own assessment."},{"p":"I named the approach I’d abandoned, the corrections that had worked, and one moment when I had let the radio distract me from the larger picture. I didn’t call any of it a disaster. I didn’t call the good landing proof of anything bigger, either."},{"p":"Mara compared my assessment with hers. We agreed on most of it. Where we differed, she asked me to explain my reasoning and showed me what she’d observed."},{"p":"Then she closed the notebook."},{"p":"“Your consistency has improved,” she said. “Not just your landings. You’re identifying problems earlier, communicating more clearly, and making better decisions about when to continue and when to change the plan.”"},{"p":"I stayed quiet, mostly because I was afraid my mouth would do something embarrassing."},{"p":"“We still have a few items to confirm in your training records and your performance,” she continued. “Your student pilot certificate and applicable medical qualification have to be in order. Before a first solo, I’ll need to verify the required training and proficiency for this make and model, complete the appropriate endorsements, and assess the conditions and your readiness that day. Nothing about today’s lesson overrides any of that.”"},{"p":"“Okay,” I said."},{"p":"“But I want you to know we’re discussing the final stages of preparation now. Not a date. A stage.”"},{"p":"My heart made one enormous thump."},{"p":"“I can live with a stage,” I said."},{"p":"“You’d better. It’s what we’re offering.”"},{"p":"Outside, Rafe was waiting near the hangar with a folded poster board under one arm. His communications demonstration was coming together, and he wanted my opinion about whether the heading was readable from across a room."},{"p":"The heading said TALKING TO AIRPLANES IS NOT THE SAME AS TALKING TO YOUR DOG."},{"p":"“Strong opening,” I told him."},{"p":"“My dog has never asked me to repeat a transmission.”"},{"p":"“Your dog also ignores half your instructions.”"},{"p":"“So does my brother. That’s not the point.”"},{"p":"We walked toward the parking lot arguing about font size, and for ten whole minutes I didn’t think about solo at all."},{"p":"That was new."},{"p":"At home, I added one sentence to the bottom of my notebook page: Today I could tell the difference between wanting to be ready and showing I was ready."},{"p":"Then I crossed out the sentence."},{"p":"It sounded too much like something printed on a poster above a school counselor’s desk."},{"p":"I tried again."},{"p":"Good flight. Still work to do. Ask Mara about the next review."},{"p":"That sounded like me."},{"p":"I shut the notebook and went downstairs to help Dad make dinner. He’d decided to cook pasta and was holding a jar of sauce as if it contained a difficult math problem."},{"p":"“Can you open this?” he asked."},{"p":"I took it, twisted, and handed it back."},{"p":"“Look at that,” he said. “Independent operation.”"},{"p":"“Please don’t tell Mara. She’ll make me do three more before she signs anything.”"},{"p":"Dad laughed so hard he nearly dropped the jar."},{"p":"For once, I didn’t mind that tomorrow was still just tomorrow."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nineteen — One Seat, One Sky","blocks":[{"p":"The morning I flew an airplane alone began with Dad burning toast."},{"p":"Not all the toast. Just the piece he’d made for me, which seemed like a personal statement from the universe."},{"p":"“It’s not burned,” he said, holding up something the color of a hockey puck. “It’s aggressively toasted.”"},{"p":"“The smoke alarm disagrees.”"},{"p":"Mom leaned into the kitchen doorway with her work bag over one shoulder. “Please don’t let the smoke alarm become our family alarm clock again.”"},{"p":"It was a Saturday, and she’d volunteered to cover a few hours at the records office. Dad had agreed to drive me to the airport afterward. Nothing about our schedule was unusual. My lesson was on the calendar, same as the one before it. Mara had said we’d review my records and evaluate the conditions before deciding what training made sense."},{"p":"She had not said the word solo."},{"p":"I had said it in my head approximately nine thousand times."},{"p":"My phone buzzed while I rescued an unburned slice from the toaster. A message from Imani appeared above a picture of her weather station’s newest chart."},{"p":"*Wind observations are not fortune-telling,* she’d written. *But today’s look less rude than yesterday’s.*"},{"p":"I sent back a thumbs-up, then deleted it because that seemed too casual, then sent a second message: *Thanks. Hope your presentation goes well.*"},{"p":"Her reply came immediately. *You know you can send one normal message without editing it like a government document, right?*"},{"p":"Apparently she knew me."},{"p":"I checked my bag before leaving: logbook, headset, notebook, water, and the folder with my student pilot paperwork. My student pilot certificate had arrived after the required application and identity-verification process, and I had already completed an examination with an aviation medical examiner and received a current third-class medical certificate. Mara had checked both documents during our earlier record reviews. Having them in the folder did not mean I could fly alone; they were two of several requirements, and she still had to assess my training, endorsements, and the conditions on the day."},{"p":"Then I checked the bag again, because checking a bag was easier than checking my stomach."},{"p":"Dad watched me from the doorway. “Did you pack a spare Lincoln?”"},{"p":"“Only the one. Budget cuts.”"},{"p":"He grinned, then softened. “Whatever happens today, it’s still a lesson.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"I thought I knew. Knowing something and believing it with your entire body were not always the same activity."},{"p":"At Hampton Roads Executive, the hangar doors stood open. A Cessna rolled slowly along the taxiway in the distance, its propeller flashing in the morning light. The air smelled faintly of damp grass and the particular combination of oil, concrete, and possibility that I’d come to associate with airports."},{"p":"Mara met me at the desk with a clipboard and no mysterious smile."},{"p":"“How are you feeling?”"},{"p":"“Like I ate toast in a moving vehicle.”"},{"p":"“Did you?”"},{"p":"“No. Dad made toast that could have been used to resurface a runway.”"},{"p":"She looked at me for a moment, then laughed. “Try the question again.”"},{"p":"I took my time. “Excited. Nervous. I slept well. Ate breakfast. I’m not sick. I’m not distracted by anything outside flying, except the fact that I’m nervous about flying.”"},{"p":"“That’s an honest answer. What does your routine tell you to do with it?”"},{"p":"“Name it. Don’t pretend it isn’t there. Then pay attention to the actual job.”"},{"p":"We sat down with my training records. Mara had already checked the airplane’s documents and maintenance status through the school’s procedures, and we reviewed the information relevant to the day’s operation. My student pilot certificate and third-class medical certificate were current. My logbook showed the required pre-solo training for the Cessna 172S, including the maneuvers and procedures she’d taught and evaluated. The pre-solo aeronautical knowledge test was complete, Mara had reviewed the questions I’d missed, and the required pre-solo knowledge endorsement was recorded. Mara discussed the applicable solo limitations, local procedures, and the current weather and airport information with me."},{"p":"She did not turn the review into a magic trick. We looked at each item because it mattered, not because it would make a dramatic ending to a chapter in my life."},{"p":"“You’re sixteen,” she said, tapping the age information on the form. “That makes you old enough for an airplane solo under the student-pilot rules. It doesn’t, by itself, make you ready.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“You’ve demonstrated the required proficiency in training, and I’ve watched your judgment become more consistent. Today I still need to see the flying and make a final decision based on the conditions we actually have.”"},{"p":"“Understood.”"},{"p":"“And if I decide not today?”"},{"p":"I swallowed. “Then not today.”"},{"p":"She studied my face, as if checking whether the words had reached the part of me that needed them."},{"p":"“Good,” she said. “Let’s go fly.”"},{"p":"We completed our preflight inspection and worked through the checklist in the cockpit. Mara didn’t hurry me. I didn’t hurry myself. Once the engine was running and we were ready, I made the appropriate radio calls and followed our usual taxi procedures. The airport looked familiar from the left seat, but the thought of what might happen later made every painted line seem especially sharp."},{"p":"Mara noticed me looking ahead for the next event instead of staying with the present one."},{"p":"“Where are we?” she asked."},{"p":"“Taxiing.”"},{"p":"“Then taxi.”"},{"p":"That was Mara. She could fit an entire lecture inside two words."},{"p":"We reviewed our plan and flew the traffic pattern together. The first landing was good, not miraculous. I kept the airplane under control, made reasonable corrections, and touched down without trying to win an award for smoothness. On the next circuit, the approach began to wander outside the stable picture we’d discussed. I recognized it early and went around. No debate, no embarrassed apology."},{"p":"“Your call,” Mara said."},{"p":"“I didn’t like the approach.”"},{"p":"“Neither did I. Good decision.”"},{"p":"The third circuit was better. The fourth was ordinary. The ordinary one mattered most, I thought, because nothing dramatic happened and I still had to do every part correctly."},{"p":"We returned to the ramp. Mara asked for my assessment before giving hers. I told her about the go-around, a moment when I could have anticipated the wind correction sooner, and the fact that I’d managed to stop judging each landing while it was still happening."},{"p":"She nodded, then checked the latest conditions again. A light breeze moved the windsock. The weather remained within the limits she’d established for my first solo, and the airport operation was suitable. She verified the required pre-solo knowledge and training endorsements, then signed the applicable current solo-flight endorsement for this make and model in my logbook, documenting the authorization and limitations. She reviewed exactly what I was permitted to do, the traffic pattern we’d agreed on, the weather and wind limits, and what to do if anything changed or felt wrong. It was a short, local flight in the same airplane—not permission to fly wherever I pleased."},{"p":"Then she said, “Lincoln, I’m satisfied you’re ready for your first supervised solo here today. Do you want to do it?”"},{"p":"For a second I heard the hum of the hangar lights more clearly than her voice."},{"p":"“Yes,” I said. Then, because I had promised myself not to perform confidence, I added, “I’m nervous. But yes.”"},{"p":"“You can be nervous and still make sound decisions. If you don’t want to go, we don’t go.”"},{"p":"“I want to.”"},{"p":"She smiled once. “All right. We’ll do one takeoff and landing in the pattern. I’ll be on the ground. Remember your training. If something isn’t right, use the options we’ve practiced.”"},{"p":"We went back to the airplane. Mara made sure the paperwork and aircraft were in order, then stepped away from the passenger door."},{"p":"It made a small, ordinary clicking sound when she closed it."},{"p":"I’d imagined that sound before. I’d expected a giant rush of excitement, perhaps a trumpet fanfare or a voice from the clouds announcing that Lincoln Burgess had finally become interesting."},{"p":"Instead, I noticed how much space one person could leave behind."},{"p":"The seat beside me was empty. The headset cord lay where Mara’s elbow usually rested. The airplane was still the same airplane. The checklist was still the checklist. The runway had not grown longer to impress me or shorter to frighten me."},{"p":"I breathed, checked my surroundings, and continued with the procedures I’d been taught. My voice sounded like mine when I transmitted on the radio, although it took an extra heartbeat to recognize it."},{"p":"On the taxiway, I thought of Dad telling me every lesson was still a lesson. Mom rearranging work hours and grocery lists to make training possible. Rafe arguing that radios were mostly a contest to see who could remain calm while sounding like they weren’t trying. Beckett explaining that a control linkage didn’t care whether you believed in it; it cared whether it worked. Imani asking better questions than I did and being brave enough to tell me when I wasn’t listening. Pike putting a blank page in front of me. Uncle Everett saying an instructor wasn’t there to make me look good."},{"p":"And Mara, sitting in that very seat through every imperfect landing, every go-around, every moment I’d wanted a shortcut."},{"p":"None of them could make this flight for me."},{"p":"Every one of them had helped me get here."},{"p":"I waited for the runway to be clear, made the appropriate call, and took my place for departure. I checked the conditions and the airplane one more time against what I’d been taught. Then I applied power and began the takeoff."},{"p":"The airplane accelerated. It felt familiar, and also different. Without Mara beside me, it was lighter. I knew to expect a difference; we’d discussed it. Knowing that didn’t make the sensation less startling. I made the necessary small corrections, kept my attention outside and on the airplane’s performance, and let it fly when it was ready."},{"p":"The wheels left the runway."},{"p":"For one astonishing moment, the entire world became very quiet inside my head."},{"p":"I was flying."},{"p":"Not pretending. Not waiting for a hand to reach across the cockpit. Not performing a story I could tell later. Just flying the airplane I’d trained to fly, in the place I’d trained to fly it, doing the work one piece at a time."},{"p":"The houses beyond the airport looked like pieces from a model set. The horizon held steady. I followed the traffic pattern and made my radio calls. My hands weren’t perfectly relaxed, but they were doing what I asked. I kept checking, looking, deciding. There was no space for a speech about how far I’d come."},{"p":"When I turned toward the final approach, I felt the old urge to make this landing special."},{"p":"I let it pass."},{"p":"A safe landing would be special enough."},{"p":"The approach developed steadily. I kept the airplane aligned and managed its energy the way Mara had taught me, making corrections while they were still small. I knew I could go around if the picture stopped making sense. That option didn’t make me less confident. It made the decision to continue an actual decision."},{"p":"The wheels touched the runway. A little firmer than my best landing. Much better than some of my earlier ones. I kept controlling the airplane, slowed, and exited safely when appropriate."},{"p":"Only then did I notice I was grinning so hard my cheeks hurt."},{"p":"Back on the ramp, after securing the airplane, I found Mara waiting a respectful distance away. She didn’t rush toward me. She let me finish what I was doing."},{"p":"“How was it?” she asked when I reached her."},{"p":"I had practiced a dozen clever answers. All of them disappeared."},{"p":"“It was quiet,” I said."},{"p":"She looked at me with an expression I couldn’t quite name."},{"p":"“Good,” she said. “Tell me about the flight.”"},{"p":"So I did. Before the cheering, before my phone, before anybody asked for a picture, we debriefed. I described the takeoff, the different feel of the airplane, my pattern, my approach, and the landing. I told her what I would keep and what I wanted to improve."},{"p":"When I finished, she said, “Congratulations, Lincoln. You earned that.”"},{"p":"I looked back at the Cessna, sitting still in the sun."},{"p":"For years I’d thought solo meant proving I could do something without help."},{"p":"Standing beside Mara, I understood that wasn’t quite right."},{"p":"It meant Mara had taught me well enough to trust my decisions when the other seat was empty."},{"p":"I hadn’t felt fearless up there."},{"p":"I had felt responsible."},{"p":"And, somehow, that was better."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty — After the Wheels Stop","blocks":[{"p":"Rafe’s first question was whether I’d remembered to bring the airplane back."},{"p":"“No,” I told him. “It’s currently orbiting the moon.”"},{"p":"“I knew it. That’s why they should have put me in charge of communications.”"},{"p":"He was standing outside the hangar with Beckett and Imani when Mara and I came out of the debrief room. I didn’t know who had told them. Probably Dad, who had been instructed to keep the morning low-key and therefore had interpreted low-key as informing everyone with a functioning phone."},{"p":"Dad was there too, holding his own phone at a suspicious angle."},{"p":"“Were you recording?” I asked."},{"p":"“Only the part where you looked like you were trying to solve a difficult algebra problem while walking.”"},{"p":"“So all of it.”"},{"p":"Mom arrived a few minutes later, still wearing her office badge. She hugged me hard enough to remind me that gravity operated on the ground as well as in the air."},{"p":"“I’m proud of you,” she said into my shoulder."},{"p":"“Thanks.”"},{"p":"“And I’m glad you landed.”"},{"p":"“That was one of my main goals.”"},{"p":"Her laugh sounded a little watery. I didn’t make a joke about that."},{"p":"Mara stood nearby, answering Dad’s questions about what the solo authorization meant. She was careful with her words. I was now a student pilot who had completed a first solo flight under specific endorsements and limitations. I wasn’t a private pilot. I wasn’t free to take passengers, fly anywhere I liked, or decide that one successful circuit had settled the rest of my training."},{"p":"I appreciated her saying it out loud, because part of me wanted to turn the morning into a shiny object and carry it around forever."},{"p":"Imani handed me a folded sheet of paper. It showed a tiny graph of the wind readings she’d collected near her house, with an arrow pointing to the morning’s gentler conditions."},{"p":"“Not an official airport weather report,” she said immediately. “Before you start using my garden equipment for flight planning.”"},{"p":"“I wouldn’t.”"},{"p":"She raised one eyebrow."},{"p":"“I would ask first,” I corrected."},{"p":"“Better.”"},{"p":"Beckett gave me a small cardboard airplane he’d assembled from scrap packaging. One wing had a pencil mark showing where he’d adjusted the balance."},{"p":"“It took three tries,” he said. “The first one flew like a brick with opinions.”"},{"p":"“So, like me during my first landing lesson.”"},{"p":"“Exactly. But with less expensive tires.”"},{"p":"Rafe had made a certificate on his computer. At the top, in enormous letters, it said CONGRATULATIONS ON SUCCESSFULLY NOT PANICKING."},{"p":"Below that, in much smaller letters, he’d added: *Also, flying an airplane alone.*"},{"p":"I laughed until I had to lean against the wall."},{"p":"We took a picture together. In the first one, Rafe was talking. In the second, Beckett blinked. In the third, Imani made us all look toward the wrong camera on purpose. Dad declared the third picture perfect, which was probably why we kept it."},{"p":"At home, the celebration involved pizza, a cake Mom had bought on the way back from work, and Uncle Everett appearing on a video call from his kitchen. He was wearing an apron with an anchor on it."},{"p":"“Commander,” I said, “are you cooking?”"},{"p":"“I am supervising a highly trained pasta operation.”"},{"p":"“Is the pasta highly trained?”"},{"p":"“More than you think. Congratulations, Lincoln.”"},{"p":"His face changed as he said it. The jokes stayed, but something serious came through underneath them."},{"p":"“How’d it feel?” he asked."},{"p":"“Different from what I expected. I thought I’d feel like I was conquering the sky. Mostly I was thinking about doing the next thing correctly.”"},{"p":"“Sounds about right.”"},{"p":"“Afterward I felt enormous,” I admitted. “And then Mara asked for the debrief, and I realized I still had things to work on.”"},{"p":"“Also sounds about right.”"},{"p":"He lifted a forkful of pasta offscreen. “The sky doesn’t hand out lifetime memberships for one good day.”"},{"p":"“Do you practice saying these things?”"},{"p":"“Only when the pasta needs motivation.”"},{"p":"After dinner, I went upstairs with the certificate, the cardboard airplane, and the little wind chart. I set them beside my logbook, where Mara had recorded the flight. I read the entry twice, partly because I liked seeing the words and partly because I couldn’t quite believe they belonged to me."},{"p":"Then I opened my notebook."},{"p":"For several minutes I wrote down everything I could remember about the flight. Not just the part where the wheels left the runway. I wrote about the checklist, the radio calls, the feeling of the airplane without Mara’s weight in the other seat, and the decision to keep the landing ordinary. I wrote about the debrief. I wrote about the moment when I had wanted to tell a better story than the one I was actually living."},{"p":"That was the part I kept coming back to."},{"p":"I had wanted my first solo to be perfect. Perfect weather, perfect takeoff, perfect landing, perfect expression on my face when I climbed out. In the version I’d imagined, nobody asked me what I could improve because there was nothing left to improve."},{"p":"The real version had surprised me."},{"p":"My landing had been safe, if not especially graceful. I’d come back with a list of things to practice."},{"p":"And nobody had been disappointed."},{"p":"A knock sounded at my door."},{"p":"“Come in.”"},{"p":"Dad leaned against the frame. “Your mother says I should check whether you’ve floated away from happiness.”"},{"p":"“Still here.”"},{"p":"He looked at the papers spread across my desk. “Working already?”"},{"p":"“Writing it down before I forget.”"},{"p":"“You won’t forget today.”"},{"p":"“Probably not. But I might forget what I learned.”"},{"p":"Dad considered that. “Fair distinction.”"},{"p":"He stepped into the room and sat on the edge of my bed. For a while he looked at the model airplane Beckett had made."},{"p":"“When you were little,” he said, “you used to run around the backyard with your arms out. You’d make engine noises until the neighbors thought we were testing a lawn mower.”"},{"p":"“I had excellent sound effects.”"},{"p":"“Terrible volume control.”"},{"p":"“Still working on that.”"},{"p":"He smiled. “I thought today would feel like the finish line for you.”"},{"p":"“It did for about five minutes.”"},{"p":"“And now?”"},{"p":"I glanced at the logbook. “Now it feels like somebody handed me something valuable and said, ‘Take care of this.’”"},{"p":"Dad nodded slowly. “That’s not a bad way to think about responsibility.”"},{"p":"“It’s a little less fun than pretending I’m an ace pilot.”"},{"p":"“You’ve always been an ace at pretending.”"},{"p":"“Thanks, Dad.”"},{"p":"He got up, then paused at the door. “For the record, I was scared today.”"},{"p":"I looked at him. Dad rarely admitted to being scared. He was the guy who read complicated library policy changes for fun and could remain calm when the washing machine flooded the hallway."},{"p":"“You were?”"},{"p":"“Of course. I trust Mara. I trust the training. And I trust that you’ve worked hard. But I’m your father. I can believe all those things and still be scared.”"},{"p":"I didn’t know what to say, so I said the truest thing I had."},{"p":"“I’m glad you came.”"},{"p":"“Wouldn’t have missed it.”"},{"p":"When he left, I turned to a fresh page. At the top I wrote: *What comes next?*"},{"p":"I listed the things Mara had already mentioned: continued dual instruction, additional solo training when authorized, more navigation work, and eventually the knowledge and practical requirements for a private pilot certificate when I was old enough and properly prepared. Nothing about the list was guaranteed. Every step would have its own standards."},{"p":"My phone buzzed again. It was Celia from the fellowship group."},{"p":"*Heard you soloed. Congratulations. Pike says you’re still responsible for the route analysis Tuesday.*"},{"p":"I laughed. *Wouldn’t dream of escaping.*"},{"p":"A moment later she replied: *Good. Dev’s spreadsheet is developing opinions.*"},{"p":"I put the phone down and looked out the window. The sky was dark now, and a few stars were visible between the neighborhood roofs. Somewhere beyond them were places I’d never been, routes I didn’t yet know how to plan, and weather I’d have to learn to understand better than I did today."},{"p":"For the first time, that didn’t feel like a list of things keeping me from becoming a pilot."},{"p":"It felt like the work of becoming one."},{"p":"I closed my notebook, switched off the desk lamp, and went downstairs to help Mom with the cake plates."},{"p":"Tomorrow, I would still be sixteen. I would still have homework. Rafe would still make terrible jokes. The airplane would still need inspecting before every flight. Mara would still expect me to explain my decisions."},{"p":"Today had changed something important."},{"p":"It hadn’t changed the rules."},{"p":"I was glad of both."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-One — The Kid with the Blue Notebook","blocks":[{"p":"On the Monday after my first solo, I discovered that the universe had not arranged a parade."},{"p":"There was a math quiz, a missing permission slip, and a mysterious stain on my favorite sweatshirt that Mom insisted looked like pizza sauce. I insisted it was probably something scientific. Neither explanation improved the sweatshirt."},{"p":"At lunch, Theo pointed his fork at me. “So. You flew an airplane by yourself.”"},{"p":"“Once,” I said."},{"p":"“Did you yell anything heroic?”"},{"p":"“I made radio calls.”"},{"p":"“That is the least heroic answer available.”"},{"p":"“I was aiming for the most boring flight possible.”"},{"p":"He thought about that. “Actually, that’s kind of heroic in reverse.”"},{"p":"Rafe, who had been quietly stealing fries from my tray, raised one. “To boring flights.”"},{"p":"“To buying your own fries,” I said."},{"p":"He ate it anyway."},{"p":"By the time I reached the airport that afternoon, my first solo had been discussed by enough people that I wanted to hide behind a fuel truck. Mara met me outside the school office holding a clipboard and wearing the expression she used when she knew exactly what I was thinking."},{"p":"“Congratulations again,” she said. “Now tell me what you’re here to do.”"},{"p":"“Continue training.”"},{"p":"“Good. The airplane doesn’t know you soloed last week.”"},{"p":"“Does it at least know I took a decent picture beside it?”"},{"p":"“It has declined to comment.”"},{"p":"We weren’t flying until later. The school’s open-house preparation had taken over the front classroom, and Mara needed help carrying folding chairs. I could manage chairs. Chairs, unlike airplanes, were generally willing to go where I pointed them."},{"p":"I had just stacked the last one when a voice behind me asked, “Are you Lincoln?”"},{"p":"I turned to find a boy who looked about twelve. He wore a navy hoodie with a tiny embroidered airplane on the sleeve and carried a bright blue notebook against his chest as if somebody might try to steal it."},{"p":"“Depends,” I said. “Did I forget to turn something in?”"},{"p":"He didn’t laugh. “I’m Milo. Mrs. Ellery said you could show me where the display boards go.”"},{"p":"“Oh. Sure.”"},{"p":"He followed me toward the classroom. Every few steps he looked through the windows at the parked airplanes. He didn’t seem shy exactly. He seemed like he was trying to look at six things at once without letting anybody notice."},{"p":"“First time here?” I asked."},{"p":"“Second. First time I came with my grandpa. He used to fix helicopters.”"},{"p":"“That’s pretty cool.”"},{"p":"“He says I ask too many questions.”"},{"p":"“Then you’ve come to a terrible place to solve that problem.”"},{"p":"That earned half a smile."},{"p":"At the front of the room, I pointed out the display boards for weather, communications, aircraft systems, and the flight-training overview. Milo immediately headed for the one showing a traffic pattern."},{"p":"“Is this what you flew by yourself?” he asked."},{"p":"“Something like it. My instructor approved one takeoff and landing in the pattern.”"},{"p":"“Wasn’t it scary?”"},{"p":"“A little.”"},{"p":"“But you knew you could do it.”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth to say yes. That would have been the neat version, the one that fit on a poster beside a picture of a smiling teenager and a shiny airplane."},{"p":"Instead I said, “I knew Mara had trained me and checked that I was ready for that particular flight. I still had to pay attention and make good decisions.”"},{"p":"Milo studied the diagram. “What if you made a mistake?”"},{"p":"“Then I’d need to recognize it and respond the way I’d been trained. And if conditions weren’t right before takeoff, I wouldn’t go.”"},{"p":"He looked disappointed. “So there wasn’t a moment when you felt like you could do anything?”"},{"p":"“No,” I said. “There was a moment when I realized I could do this one thing.”"},{"p":"He wrote something in his notebook."},{"p":"“What’d you put?”"},{"p":"“ *One thing.*”"},{"p":"I hoped he’d written down the part about preparation, too. Seven minutes into our friendship seemed early for a lecture."},{"p":"A few minutes later, Mara asked me to help Milo set up a table where visitors could practice reading a simplified, clearly labeled training chart. It wasn’t an actual flight-planning station. The routes were invented, and the point was to ask good questions before making a recommendation."},{"p":"I gave Milo the sample map. “Where would you go?”"},{"p":"He traced a line with his finger. “Here. Shortest way.”"},{"p":"“Okay. What would you want to know before deciding?”"},{"p":"He stared at the page. “How much fuel?”"},{"p":"“Good question.”"},{"p":"“The weather?”"},{"p":"“Another good one.”"},{"p":"“Is there something wrong with the route?”"},{"p":"“Maybe. What on the page makes you wonder?”"},{"p":"He pointed to a shaded circle near the line. I asked him to read the legend. We found that the circle represented an area with a special restriction in our pretend scenario. Milo changed his route and looked proud."},{"p":"Then I made my first mistake of the afternoon."},{"p":"“That route avoids the marked area,” I said. “What would you check next?”"},{"p":"Milo’s shoulders dropped. “So I was wrong before.”"},{"p":"“We hadn’t checked the legend yet,” I said. “That’s why we check.”"},{"p":"He looked up. “But you already knew the answer.”"},{"p":"I glanced at the answer sheet lying facedown on the table. “I knew what the exercise designer wanted us to notice. That’s not the same as knowing every answer to a real flight.”"},{"p":"He considered this for a long moment, then asked, “Can I make a harder one?”"},{"p":"“Absolutely.”"},{"p":"He added a pretend storm cloud to the map, drew a second airport, and invented a passenger who needed to be home for dinner. He called the passenger “the mayor,” which seemed unnecessarily stressful."},{"p":"“What would you do?” he asked."},{"p":"“I’d ask whether the mayor had considered a sandwich.”"},{"p":"Milo laughed so suddenly he dropped his pencil."},{"p":"We worked through the made-up situation without pretending a chart alone could settle it. By the end, he had covered two notebook pages with questions. He still didn’t have one perfect answer. He was already drawing another route before I could put the map away."},{"p":"When Mara came over, he told her, “Lincoln doesn’t tell you what to think.”"},{"p":"Mara looked at me. “Doesn’t he?”"},{"p":"“Not much,” Milo said."},{"p":"“Remarkable development,” she replied."},{"p":"After Milo left to find his grandfather, I followed Mara out to the ramp. The afternoon sun made the parked Cessna look almost white."},{"p":"“He’s a good kid,” I said."},{"p":"“He is. And you did well with him.”"},{"p":"“I almost turned into a walking answer key.”"},{"p":"“You caught yourself.”"},{"p":"“Barely.”"},{"p":"“That’s often how catching yourself starts.”"},{"p":"We stood for a moment watching a line of clouds move slowly across the distant sky."},{"p":"“It’s strange,” I said. “He thought I knew everything because I soloed.”"},{"p":"“Did you correct him?”"},{"p":"“I tried.”"},{"p":"“Then remember how that felt. People will sometimes give you more authority than you’ve earned. It’s your job not to borrow the extra.”"},{"p":"I looked down at my hands. They were still dusty from the folding chairs."},{"p":"A week earlier, I’d been thinking about the empty seat beside me. Now Milo was watching what I did with the seat across the table."},{"p":"Before our lesson, Mara reviewed the plan with me. We would continue dual instruction and work on the next training objectives. My first solo authorization hadn’t become a blanket permission to take an airplane whenever I liked. She said it in her ordinary voice, which somehow made the point land harder."},{"p":"I checked the weather with her, reviewed the aircraft information, and asked about a wind change I didn’t like. We adjusted the plan. During the flight, one landing was smooth and the next was merely acceptable. I made a small correction too late on one approach and told Mara about it before she had to ask."},{"p":"Back on the ground, she closed the logbook. “What do you think?”"},{"p":"“Not my best day.”"},{"p":"“Was it a useful day?”"},{"p":"I thought of Milo’s blue notebook, his questions, and the shaded circle on the pretend chart."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “I think it was.”"},{"p":"“Good. We’ll keep going.”"},{"p":"I walked to the parking lot with chalk dust on my sleeve and a fresh correction in my notebook. Tomorrow, Milo would probably have six more questions."},{"p":"I felt less like a newly famous pilot and more like a student who had learned something worth keeping."},{"p":"For once, that felt like enough."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-Two — The Plan Everybody Loved","blocks":[{"p":"The academy open house was supposed to be a simple Saturday event."},{"p":"That was what Rafe said on Tuesday."},{"p":"By Thursday, he had written a communications demonstration script, rewritten it twice, and threatened to resign from an organization that did not employ him."},{"p":"“It’s a five-minute demonstration,” I told him as we carried a box of printed programs into the school office."},{"p":"“Five minutes is a lifetime when a microphone squeals.”"},{"p":"“You’re the communications expert.”"},{"p":"“That’s why I know what can go wrong.”"},{"p":"Beckett emerged from the maintenance display area with a cardboard model of a flight-control linkage. He had built a handle that moved a little flap on the model wing. It looked sturdy enough to survive a classroom full of curious fingers, which was the engineering equivalent of surviving a hurricane."},{"p":"“Do not touch the red lever,” he told Rafe."},{"p":"Rafe touched the red lever."},{"p":"The flap moved. Nothing exploded."},{"p":"Beckett stared at him. “I wanted to see if you’d listen.”"},{"p":"“Your test results are in,” I said. “He won’t.”"},{"p":"Across the room, Imani was arranging a small weather display. Her home-built observation project had grown into a tidy board of measurements, photographs, and explanations. She had included a section titled *What One Reading Can’t Tell You*, which I suspected was her polite way of telling people not to build a forecast out of one glance at the sky."},{"p":"“How’s Nana June?” I asked."},{"p":"“Better this week. She sent a picture of the tomatoes.”"},{"p":"“Did they survive?”"},{"p":"“Three did. She says that’s a winning season.”"},{"p":"“She’s not wrong.”"},{"p":"Imani handed me a roll of tape. “Can you put this up straight?”"},{"p":"I looked at the display. “You are trusting me with a horizontal line?”"},{"p":"“I’m taking a calculated risk.”"},{"p":"The four of us had spent so much time together over the past few years that I sometimes forgot we weren’t all trying to reach the same destination. Rafe could make a radio demonstration interesting to people who thought aviation meant loud engines. Beckett could explain why a tiny mechanical part mattered without making anyone feel stupid. Imani could turn a cloud photograph into a question you wanted to answer."},{"p":"And me? I was still figuring that out."},{"p":"On Saturday morning, the open house filled with families, students, a few local pilots, and at least one person who appeared to have come exclusively for the cookies. Milo arrived with his grandfather, who shook my hand as though I’d been hired to manage an airline."},{"p":"“He’s been talking about the map exercise all week,” his grandfather said."},{"p":"Milo held up the blue notebook. “I made three more.”"},{"p":"“That’s fantastic,” I said. “You should show them to Mara.”"},{"p":"He looked past me toward the airplanes. “Can you take me flying today?”"},{"p":"My stomach gave a small, unpleasant twist."},{"p":"I could have said something vague and exciting. I could have made a promise about someday, or acted like my new solo status gave me the right to offer rides."},{"p":"Instead I said, “No. I’m a student pilot, and my solo privileges are limited to the training I’m authorized to do. I can’t take passengers. But we can ask Mara what introductory opportunities the school offers with an appropriately qualified instructor.”"},{"p":"Milo nodded, a little disappointed."},{"p":"His grandfather looked pleased. “That’s a good answer.”"},{"p":"“It took me a second,” I admitted."},{"p":"“Good answers sometimes do.”"},{"p":"Later, Rafe’s demonstration went almost perfectly. The microphone crackled once, which he handled by announcing, “And that concludes our demonstration of equipment having opinions.” People laughed, and he recovered without losing his place."},{"p":"Beckett’s display drew a cluster of younger kids who took turns moving the model flap. He patiently explained what the linkage represented, then asked them what might happen if it jammed. One girl answered, “You’d be really annoyed,” and Beckett said, “That is technically true.”"},{"p":"Imani’s weather station attracted more adults than any of us expected. She explained why local observations were useful but limited, and why a pilot needed current, appropriate aviation weather information rather than one backyard sensor. When a visitor asked whether her device could predict tomorrow’s thunderstorms, she answered, “Not responsibly.”"},{"p":"I grinned. That sounded exactly like her."},{"p":"At my chart table, Milo helped another kid notice a symbol in the legend. He didn’t give away the answer. He asked, “What else do we need to know?”"},{"p":"I watched him turn the map so the younger kid could read the legend."},{"p":"Near noon, Captain Pike arrived in a plain jacket, carrying a folder instead of the mysterious black briefcase I had once imagined all serious evaluators owned. He watched Rafe’s demonstration, asked Beckett a thoughtful question, and spent several minutes at Imani’s display."},{"p":"Then he came to my table."},{"p":"“Burgess.”"},{"p":"“Captain Pike.”"},{"p":"“Your group presentation was solid.”"},{"p":"“Thank you.”"},{"p":"He watched Milo explaining the fictional chart to two younger visitors. “And I see you’ve found an assistant.”"},{"p":"“He found me, mostly.”"},{"p":"Pike’s expression softened almost enough to count as a smile. “That’s often how it works.”"},{"p":"He placed the folder on the table. “The fellowship has been invited to take part in a regional youth aviation planning exercise next month. More demanding than our classroom scenarios. The teams will prepare recommendations for a supervised educational flight program, and professional pilots will review their work before any operational decision.”"},{"p":"I leaned closer. “Actual flight planning?”"},{"p":"“Actual planning standards. Not actual authority to dispatch an aircraft.”"},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"“There’s a difference, and I expect you to remember it.”"},{"p":"I opened the folder. The first page described a coastal route with several proposed stops, student observation assignments, and a schedule that depended on arriving at the final location before an afternoon program began. Before I finished the first paragraph, I was already tracing possible routes with my finger."},{"p":"“Your team may be selected to present its recommendation to the organizing committee,” Pike said. “The committee wants a workable plan. I want an honest one.”"},{"p":"“Those should be the same thing.”"},{"p":"“They should.”"},{"p":"He tapped the folder once. “Review the material with Celia, Nora, and Dev. Don’t promise an answer until you’ve verified what you need. I will meet with your team on Monday.”"},{"p":"Then he left to speak with Mara."},{"p":"For the next hour, I helped visitors and tried not to stare at the folder. When the last family departed, the four of us gathered around Imani’s weather display, eating cookies that had somehow survived the crowds."},{"p":"“Look at us,” Rafe said. “A functioning aviation event.”"},{"p":"“Mostly functioning,” Beckett replied. “Someone tried to remove my red lever.”"},{"p":"“Was it Rafe?” I asked."},{"p":"“No. Rafe tried to remove the blue one.”"},{"p":"“I contain multitudes,” Rafe said."},{"p":"Imani took a bite of cookie. “How was your chart table?”"},{"p":"“Good. Milo taught two kids to ask questions instead of guessing.”"},{"p":"“Sounds like somebody else I know,” she said."},{"p":"I handed Milo another pencil and tried not to look too pleased."},{"p":"That evening, I sat at my desk with the folder open and my notebook beside it. The event had been a success. My friends had each done something worth being proud of. I’d helped a younger student without pretending to be more qualified than I was. It should have been an easy night to feel satisfied."},{"p":"Then I reached the schedule on page four."},{"p":"The organizers had built the whole day around a tight sequence of arrivals and departures. The proposed route crossed several areas where airspace and airport information would need careful checking, and one stop had almost no room in the schedule for a delay. The packet included a preliminary chart excerpt and a note saying the plan had already received enthusiastic support."},{"p":"Enthusiastic support was not a source of aeronautical information."},{"p":"I listed the questions we needed answered: which current publications and notices the committee was using, and what alternatives existed if a stop became unsuitable. I couldn’t verify everything from the packet. That was the problem."},{"p":"A message from Celia appeared on my phone."},{"p":"*Just got the packet. Looks ambitious.*"},{"p":"I typed, *Page four worries me.*"},{"p":"*The schedule?*"},{"p":"*The assumptions underneath it.*"},{"p":"Three dots appeared, vanished, then appeared again."},{"p":"*Dev says the committee already announced the route.*"},{"p":"I looked at the packet’s cheerful cover page. The headline called the program a celebration of young aviators and their future. There was even a drawing of an airplane following a bright blue line over the coast."},{"p":"I liked the drawing. I liked the idea of the event. I liked the thought of our team being trusted with something bigger than a classroom exercise."},{"p":"But the line on the cover wasn’t a flight plan."},{"p":"My phone rang. It was Celia."},{"p":"“Tell me you’re not about to send Pike a seven-page complaint,” she said without saying hello."},{"p":"“Only six pages. I cut the introduction.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“I haven’t sent anything. I want us to look at the current information together.”"},{"p":"She was quiet for a moment. “If we find a problem, the organizers might have to change the event.”"},{"p":"“Maybe.”"},{"p":"“And if we don’t find one?”"},{"p":"“Then we can explain why the plan works, with evidence.”"},{"p":"“Okay,” she said. “Let’s do that.”"},{"p":"After we hung up, I opened a new notebook page. At the top I wrote three headings: *What we know. What we need. Who decides.*"},{"p":"Under the last one, I wrote: *Not us alone.*"},{"p":"A minute later, an email arrived from the fellowship office. The subject line read: **REGIONAL PROGRAM — ROUTE ANNOUNCEMENT**."},{"p":"The opening sentence thanked the planning team for supporting an itinerary that would be shared with participating families. It asked for student recommendations by Monday, but the public announcement would go out first thing Sunday morning."},{"p":"I checked the time. Saturday, 8:43 p.m."},{"p":"If we found a reason the plan needed changing, we might be telling people something they didn’t want to hear after they’d already made promises."},{"p":"I looked at the photograph of my first solo beside my logbook. In the picture, I was smiling so hard my face barely looked like mine. I’d spent years wanting the freedom that photograph represented."},{"p":"Now I was staring at a different kind of responsibility: not the ability to make a decision alone, but the willingness to speak up when a decision involved other people."},{"p":"I sent Celia one final message."},{"p":"*Let’s verify the facts before the announcement. I’ll ask Pike what information the organizers have that we don’t.*"},{"p":"Then I shut the folder."},{"p":"I pictured families reading the announcement over breakfast, already making plans around that bright blue line."},{"p":"And unlike the first solo, this was not a flight I could practice in advance."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-Three — Before the Announcement","blocks":[{"p":"At 7:06 Sunday morning, Celia called while I was making toast."},{"p":"My phone buzzed against the counter so hard it traveled toward the sink. I caught it with a butter knife, which was not a technique I intended to demonstrate at the academy."},{"p":"“Please tell me you’re awake,” she said."},{"p":"“I’m awake. My toast might not survive.”"},{"p":"“The announcement is scheduled for nine.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Dev found something.”"},{"p":"I set down the knife. Behind me, Dad was reading a library book with a receipt sticking out of it. He glanced up at the tone of my voice."},{"p":"“Something real,” Celia added. “Not a feeling.”"},{"p":"“Tell me.”"},{"p":"“It’s about the middle stop. The airport listed in the committee’s schedule.”"},{"p":"She read me the identifier. It was a small airport I’d looked at twice the night before, once on the packet’s printed map and once on a current chart. The route alone hadn’t told me whether the stop would work. That was why my notebook held questions instead of accusations."},{"p":"“The packet says the group will use the paved runway,” Celia said. “Dev checked the current airport information. The paved runway isn’t listed at the length in their packet.”"},{"p":"“How different?”"},{"p":"“Enough that we shouldn’t pretend it’s a typo.”"},{"p":"“Is the information current?”"},{"p":"“That’s what we’re trying to confirm.”"},{"p":"I reached for my notebook. On its newest page were the headings I’d written the night before: *What we know. What we need. Who decides.*"},{"p":"“I’ll call Pike,” I said."},{"p":"“I already emailed him. Nora is checking the publication date. Dev is checking whether the packet might describe a different runway.”"},{"p":"“Okay. Good.”"},{"p":"There was a pause."},{"p":"“Lincoln?”"},{"p":"“Yeah?”"},{"p":"“You’re not going to call the committee yourself, right?”"},{"p":"“Not before we have a verified fact and Pike knows.”"},{"p":"“Good. Because last year you might have.”"},{"p":"I looked at my reflection in the microwave door. My hair was doing something that required its own weather advisory."},{"p":"“Last year I might have written a dramatic email with seven bullet points.”"},{"p":"“Only seven?”"},{"p":"“Eight if I was feeling helpful.”"},{"p":"She laughed once, then grew serious again. “I’ll call when Nora gets back to us.”"},{"p":"Dad closed his book after I hung up."},{"p":"“School project?” he asked."},{"p":"“Fellowship. There’s a public event, and a route might be based on outdated airport information.”"},{"p":"“Do you know it’s outdated?”"},{"p":"“Not yet.”"},{"p":"He nodded. “Then it sounds like you know what to do next.”"},{"p":"I had spent years wishing adults would stop answering questions with questions. Now I was discovering it was a contagious habit."},{"p":"By eight, our team had a video call going. Celia was at her kitchen table with a mug the size of a soup bowl. Dev’s camera pointed at the ceiling until Nora told him we were trying to solve an airport problem, not inspect his light fixture."},{"p":"Nora shared her screen. She had the current FAA airport data in one window and the committee’s packet in another. The runway details did not match."},{"p":"“Before anybody says the packet is wrong,” Nora said, “we should make sure we’re comparing the same runway and the same units.”"},{"p":"Dev leaned toward his camera. “Same runway designation. Same units. I checked twice.”"},{"p":"“Show us,” Celia said."},{"p":"He did. The packet had carried forward a runway length from an older planning sheet. The current published information showed a shorter available paved runway. The difference mattered because the committee’s itinerary assumed that stop was suitable for every participating aircraft, without showing aircraft-specific performance planning."},{"p":"I felt a familiar little spark: the excitement of finding the answer."},{"p":"Then I caught myself."},{"p":"We hadn’t found *the* answer. We’d found a fact that changed the question."},{"p":"“Let’s write exactly what we verified,” I said. “And what we haven’t.”"},{"p":"Celia looked up from her notes. “Go on.”"},{"p":"“The published runway information doesn’t match the committee packet. We can support that. We can’t decide whether a particular airplane can use the airport without the airplane’s performance data, actual conditions, and a qualified pilot’s assessment. And we don’t know whether the committee already has a revised plan.”"},{"p":"“That’s less satisfying than saying we saved the day,” Dev said."},{"p":"“I’m working on becoming a disappointment to dramatic people.”"},{"p":"“It’s going well.”"},{"p":"Nora typed our finding into a shared document. She attached the publication details and marked the older packet figure as needing correction. Celia added a request that the organizers verify the stop with the participating pilots and appropriate airport information before treating it as confirmed."},{"p":"At 8:17, Pike joined the call. He listened without interrupting, which was somehow more intimidating than when he interrupted."},{"p":"When we finished, he asked, “Who decided the stop is unusable?”"},{"p":"“No one,” I said. “We haven’t established that.”"},{"p":"“Who decided the original plan is acceptable?”"},{"p":"“We don’t know,” Celia answered. “The packet doesn’t include the supporting checks.”"},{"p":"“And what can you tell the committee?”"},{"p":"Nora read our short finding aloud. It contained no heroic language, no guess about a particular airplane, and no sentence beginning with *Obviously*."},{"p":"Pike gave one small nod."},{"p":"“Send it to me and the program coordinator. I’ll request that the public itinerary describe the stop as provisional until the operational review is complete.”"},{"p":"Dev exhaled. “So the announcement might change.”"},{"p":"“It might,” Pike said. “The purpose of your work is to make that possibility visible before someone treats an assumption as a promise.”"},{"p":"“Will they be angry?” I asked."},{"p":"“Possibly.”"},{"p":"It wasn’t the reassuring answer I’d been hoping for."},{"p":"Pike folded his hands. “A professional recommendation doesn’t become less necessary because it’s inconvenient. It does, however, need to be accurate, proportionate, and delivered to the people responsible for acting on it.”"},{"p":"After the call, our document went out with four names at the bottom. Not just mine. I was pleased about that in a way I hadn’t expected."},{"p":"At 9:02, the event announcement appeared. It listed the cities, the purpose of the youth program, and the proposed activities. The middle airport stop was labeled *pending final operational review*."},{"p":"I read the sentence three times."},{"p":"One sentence in the announcement now matched what we actually knew. I read it again, just to be sure."},{"p":"By lunchtime, my stomach had stopped trying to tie itself into a knot."},{"p":"Then an email arrived from the program coordinator. The subject line was **REGIONAL PROGRAM — REVIEW TEAM REQUEST**."},{"p":"She thanked us for identifying the discrepancy. The organizers wanted our group to attend a meeting with the adult flight-planning advisers later that week. The itinerary would remain provisional while they reviewed the stop and the larger schedule."},{"p":"At the bottom was a question: *Would Lincoln Burgess be willing to present the student team’s findings?*"},{"p":"I stared at my name."},{"p":"Six months ago, I would have said yes before reaching the end of the sentence. A year ago, I might have imagined standing in front of a room, pointing at a chart, and watching everyone realize how much I knew."},{"p":"Now I thought about Celia’s questions, Nora’s source checks, Dev’s careful comparison, and Pike’s reminder about who made the decisions."},{"p":"I called Celia."},{"p":"“They asked me to present,” I said."},{"p":"“I saw.”"},{"p":"“I think I should ask for all four of us to present our parts.”"},{"p":"“Really?”"},{"p":"“You did the coordination. Nora checked the source. Dev caught the mismatch. I helped frame what it meant. If they want the team’s findings, they should hear the team.”"},{"p":"She was quiet long enough that I checked whether the call had dropped."},{"p":"“Celia?”"},{"p":"“I’m here,” she said. “That’s a good idea.”"},{"p":"“It’s also four times as many opportunities for someone to spill water on the projector.”"},{"p":"“Then we’re definitely doing it.”"},{"p":"I wrote back to the coordinator and copied my teammates. I thanked her, explained that we could present together, and asked whether the adult advisers could share the current proposed operating assumptions ahead of the meeting. We wanted to be prepared to answer questions, but we weren’t going to pretend to approve a flight operation."},{"p":"The reply arrived that afternoon: *Yes. Please come as a team.*"},{"p":"For a while I sat at my desk with the message open. Beside my keyboard was the photograph from my first solo. In it, I looked delighted and slightly startled, as though the airplane had surprised me by doing exactly what Mara had trained me to do."},{"p":"That day, I had been responsible for one airplane on one carefully limited flight."},{"p":"This time there were aircraft I hadn’t seen and people I hadn’t met. I could bring the source documents and the questions; the adult advisers would have to make the operational decisions."},{"p":"A message appeared from Rafe."},{"p":"*Heard you’re saving aviation again. Can I be your public- address announcer?*"},{"p":"I typed back, *Nobody’s saving anything. We corrected a runway detail.*"},{"p":"*Great. My opening line will be: IN A WORLD WHERE DETAILS MATTER…*"},{"p":"I smiled and set the phone down."},{"p":"The committee still had a difficult decision ahead. The route might change. The schedule might change. People might be disappointed. Our team would have to explain its work in a room where the stakes were higher than a classroom exercise."},{"p":"I couldn’t tell how that meeting would go."},{"p":"But for once, I didn’t need to pretend I could."},{"p":"I opened my notebook and turned to a clean page."},{"p":"At the top, I wrote: *Questions for the people who decide.*"},{"p":"Then I started preparing."}]},{"t":"Epilogue — Four Different Directions","blocks":[{"p":"The next Saturday, the four of us sat on the grass beyond the academy’s picnic tables with paper cups of lemonade and a bag of pretzels Rafe insisted were “aviation fuel for people.”"},{"p":"The sky was bright enough to make me squint. A Cessna taxied past the hangars, its propeller flashing in the sun. I watched it until Beckett nudged my shoe."},{"p":"“You’re doing the thing,” he said."},{"p":"“What thing?”"},{"p":"“Looking at an airplane like it’s about to explain your entire future.”"},{"p":"“Maybe it knows something.”"},{"p":"“It doesn’t even know where the parking spots are. The pilot just missed one.”"},{"p":"Sure enough, the airplane rolled slowly past an open tie-down spot and stopped farther along. I laughed."},{"p":"Rafe raised his cup. “To being human.”"},{"p":"“An alarming toast,” Imani said, but she lifted hers too."},{"p":"We’d come to the academy to help pack away the open-house signs. That job had taken twenty minutes. We’d been sitting in the grass for almost an hour, which suggested none of us was eager to go home yet."},{"p":"“So,” Rafe said, “what’s everybody doing when we’re famous?”"},{"p":"“I’m not planning to be famous,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“That’s what makes your rise so compelling.”"},{"p":"Beckett brushed pretzel salt from his jeans. “I want to work on aircraft. Maybe design parts. Maybe figure out why the parts other people design don’t work the way they say they will.”"},{"p":"“You’ll need a very large complaint department,” I said."},{"p":"“I’ll call it Engineering.”"},{"p":"Imani tipped her face toward the wind. “I’d like to study weather properly. Not just collect observations and make guesses. I want to understand why forecasts are hard, and how to make them more useful.”"},{"p":"“Especially for tomato farmers,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Nana June would approve.”"},{"p":"“And you?” I asked him."},{"p":"“Communications,” he said immediately. “Maybe air traffic control someday. Or something where I get to help people make sense of what’s happening without being the one flying the airplane.”"},{"p":"“That sounds exactly like you,” I said."},{"p":"“Thank you. I accept compliments in cash or snacks.”"},{"p":"Then all three looked at me."},{"p":"“Pilot,” I said. “I still want that. But I don’t know exactly what kind yet.”"},{"p":"Rafe gasped. “Lincoln Trace Burgess admitting uncertainty in public. Somebody alert the news.”"},{"p":"“I’m expanding my range.”"},{"p":"“You used to have one setting,” Imani said. “Confidently explaining things.”"},{"p":"“Two settings,” Beckett corrected. “Confidently explaining things while walking into a chair.”"},{"p":"“That happened once.”"},{"p":"“Twice,” said Rafe."},{"p":"I threw a pretzel at him. It missed, which seemed fair."},{"p":"A moment later, Imani leaned back on her elbows. “It’s strange, though. When we first started coming here, I thought we’d all end up doing the same thing.”"},{"p":"“Me too,” I admitted."},{"p":"“And now?” she asked."},{"p":"I looked at Beckett’s hands, always turning something over to see how it worked. At Rafe, who could turn an awkward silence into a conversation. At Imani, who noticed changes in the sky before the rest of us thought to look up."},{"p":"“Now I think it’d be a waste if we did.”"},{"p":"Nobody made a speech about that. Rafe simply passed the pretzels around again."},{"p":"My phone buzzed with a reminder about the regional planning meeting. For an instant, the familiar nerves returned. The adult advisers would ask questions. The team would need to be clear about what we’d verified and what we hadn’t. We might walk out without a final answer."},{"p":"I put the phone away."},{"p":"“Big meeting?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“You ready?”"},{"p":"I considered it. There were things I couldn’t control, including the committee’s decision and whether the presentation room contained a working projector. But our sources were organized, our questions were honest, and my teammates knew their parts."},{"p":"“Ready to explain what we know,” I said. “Not ready to pretend we know the rest.”"},{"p":"Imani smiled. “That sounds like progress.”"},{"p":"Across the field, Mara stepped out of the hangar and waved. I waved back. I remembered the first time I’d sat beside her in an airplane, certain that becoming a pilot meant eventually not needing anybody in the other seat."},{"p":"I’d flown alone now. The memory still made my chest feel strangely light."},{"p":"But the empty seat hadn’t erased the people who taught me. And it hadn’t changed what my friends brought to the table."},{"p":"The airplane at the far end of the ramp began to move again. Its wings caught the afternoon light as it rolled toward the taxiway."},{"p":"I didn’t know where it was headed."},{"p":"I watched until it turned the corner, then reached into the bag for another pretzel."},{"p":"We still had an entire afternoon ahead of us."}]},{"t":"Ground School Notes — Solo Means Responsibility","blocks":[{"p":"Lincoln’s first solo is a milestone in flight training, not the end of learning. These notes describe general United States aviation concepts in accessible language. They are educational background, not instructions for operating an airplane. Actual requirements depend on the aircraft, operation, regulations, and an instructor’s assessment. Always consult current FAA guidance and qualified instructors."},{"h":"1. Sixteen Is an Eligibility Milestone, Not a Solo Appointment"},{"p":"Under U.S. rules, a student generally must be **at least sixteen years old to solo a powered airplane**. Lincoln reaches that age before his first solo. A birthday alone doesn’t authorize the flight. He must meet the other applicable requirements and show his instructor that he can operate safely in the circumstances involved."},{"p":"For a private pilot certificate in an airplane, the general minimum age is **seventeen**. Soloing at sixteen does not make Lincoln a private pilot. He is still a student pilot learning under appropriate supervision and restrictions."},{"p":"**Think about it:** What is the difference between being old enough to attempt a responsibility and being ready to carry it out?"},{"h":"2. Student Pilot Certificate and Medical Qualification"},{"p":"Before soloing an airplane, a student must hold an appropriate **student pilot certificate** and meet the applicable **medical qualification** requirements. For Lincoln’s training in a Cessna 172S, the story assumes Mara and the school have verified these qualifications before authorizing his solo."},{"p":"Different kinds of flying can involve different rules. Students should never assume that a document or qualification for one activity automatically applies to another."},{"p":"**Try this:** Make two columns labeled *Eligible* and *Ready*. List examples from sports, school, or another activity. Notice how the columns overlap without meaning exactly the same thing."},{"h":"3. Training, Knowledge, and Instructor Endorsements"},{"p":"An airplane student pilot must receive and demonstrate required training before solo flight. Under **14 CFR § 61.87**, the student must satisfactorily complete a pre-solo aeronautical knowledge test administered and reviewed by an authorized instructor, receive the applicable flight training, demonstrate satisfactory proficiency and safety, and obtain the required endorsements."},{"p":"That pre-solo test is **not** the FAA private pilot knowledge examination. The tests serve different purposes at different stages of training."},{"p":"The instructor’s endorsement for solo in a particular make and model is subject to regulatory limitations, including a **90-day** validity period for the applicable solo authorization. A student cannot treat an old endorsement as permission to fly indefinitely."},{"p":"**Think about it:** Why might an instructor want recent evidence of skill rather than relying only on something a student did months earlier?"},{"h":"4. The First Solo Has Limits"},{"p":"A student pilot flying solo cannot carry passengers. Solo flight is subject to instructor endorsements, operating limitations, and other applicable rules, including those governing weather and airspace. Lincoln’s first solo is a carefully limited local flight—not permission to take friends sightseeing or fly anywhere he chooses."},{"p":"Mara remains responsible for providing appropriate instruction and deciding whether to authorize the specific training activity. Lincoln, as the person operating the airplane alone, must also follow the rules and make sound decisions within his authorization."},{"p":"**Think about it:** Why does being trusted with a limited responsibility not automatically grant permission for a bigger one?"},{"h":"5. A Go-Around Is a Normal Safety Decision"},{"p":"A landing attempt does not have to end in a landing. If an approach becomes unsuitable or the pilot cannot maintain a safe, stabilized approach, a go-around may be the appropriate choice. Lincoln learns that choosing not to force a landing is evidence of judgment, not failure."},{"p":"Exact aircraft procedures are learned with a qualified instructor using the appropriate flight manual and training guidance. These notes deliberately do not provide step-by-step operating instructions."},{"p":"**Try this:** Think of a time you chose to restart a task rather than force a poor result. What information helped you decide?"},{"h":"6. Nerves, Practice, and Consistency"},{"p":"A single excellent lesson can feel like a breakthrough. But readiness is shown by repeatable performance, good judgment, and the ability to recognize when something is not going well. Lincoln develops a preparation routine that helps him pay attention to the task rather than the pressure to impress someone."},{"p":"Good instructors do more than watch whether a maneuver works once. They observe how a student prepares, notices changing conditions, responds to mistakes, and makes decisions across different lessons."},{"p":"**Try this:** Keep a practice log for a skill you are learning. Instead of writing only *great* or *terrible*, record one thing you did well and one thing to improve."},{"h":"7. Published Airport Information Matters"},{"p":"In the regional planning challenge, Lincoln’s team finds a mismatch between a committee packet and current published airport information. A runway’s published characteristics can matter to aircraft performance planning, but knowing one number does not establish whether a particular aircraft can safely operate there."},{"p":"Pilots and flight planners must consider the actual aircraft, loading, conditions, runway information, and other relevant factors. The youth team can verify and report a discrepancy. It cannot replace the judgment and responsibility of the qualified adults planning and conducting the flights."},{"p":"**Think about it:** What is the difference between identifying a missing fact and making a final decision based on all the facts?"},{"h":"8. Who Makes the Decision?"},{"p":"Aviation relies on clear roles. An instructor teaches and evaluates students. A pilot in command has defined responsibilities for an actual flight. Event organizers manage their program, but enthusiasm or a public announcement does not override aviation requirements."},{"p":"Lincoln’s team helps by asking what is known, what still needs verification, and who has authority to decide. Those questions are useful far beyond aviation."},{"p":"**Try this:** Imagine your class is organizing a field trip. Write down which facts students can research, which decisions teachers or parents must make, and which details should be confirmed before announcing the plan."},{"h":"Lincoln’s Logbook Challenge — The Three Questions"},{"p":"Choose a decision from your week. Before making it, write down:"},{"p":"1. **What do I know?** Separate facts from guesses."},{"p":"2. **What do I need to check?** Identify the missing information."},{"p":"3. **Who should decide?** Consider whether you have the authority and experience, or whether someone else must be involved."},{"p":"Lincoln learns that independence does not mean knowing everything or acting alone. It means using the preparation you’ve earned, recognizing your limits, and taking responsibility for your choices."},{"p":"**A final reminder:** These notes are a story companion. For real-world aviation training and regulatory questions, use current FAA publications and the guidance of an appropriately qualified flight instructor."},{"h":"AVIATION NOTE"},{"p":"Aviation procedures, regulations, publications, equipment, and airport information can change. This series is fiction and is not a substitute for flight instruction, current official publications, aircraft operating information, or the judgment of a qualified instructor or pilot in command. Readers interested in learning to fly should use current FAA materials and instruction appropriate to their training."},{"h":"HYPE TEEN FLIGHT ACADEMY"},{"p":"Hype Teen Flight Academy follows Lincoln Burgess and his friends as they grow from curious beginners into young aviators who understand that good flying begins long before takeoff. Across seven books, real aviation concepts become part of stories about friendship, judgment, preparation, setbacks, independence, leadership, and service."},{"p":"1. *Balance Before Control*"},{"p":"2. *Crosswind Decisions*"},{"p":"3. *Weather Minimums*"},{"p":"4. *Cross-Country*"},{"p":"5. *Solo*"},{"p":"6. *Pilot in Command*"},{"p":"7. *The Sky We Share*"}],"links":[{"a":"unit:ppl","t":"Private Pilot Basics"},{"a":"unit:landings","t":"Takeoffs + Landings"},{"a":"unit:adm","t":"Decisions + Risk"}]}],"words":34838}); })();
